// Testing/SpecParser.maxon — parse a `specs-shv2/*.md` spec file into a list of
// runnable tests. Trimmed HARD from v1's SpecParser: shv2 needs only the
// `<!-- test: NAME -->` marker, the ```maxon source block, and one expected
// block (```exitcode or ```maxoncstderr), plus the `status:` frontmatter. Every
// v1 feature the specs-shv2 corpus does not use — fragment caching, RequiredIR /
// RequiredLowering, the `TimeoutMs` directive, doc-example extraction — is dropped; each
// returns when a spec first needs it. **Per-target `Stdout:<target>` was the first
// one to have come back** — BATCH2 slice 3, for `specs/filepath.md`, whose 26
// separator-printing cases pin one answer per platform (see `TargetStdoutFencePrefix`, and
// `TargetCompilerErrorFencePrefix` for the two twins it has since grown, which do the same for a
// COMPILER diagnostic and for a run's stderr) —
// **`RequiredRdata` is the second**, at BATCH8, where the read-only section became the
// second thing no golden could see (see `RequiredRdataFence`), and **`Args` is the third**,
// for the 51 `/specs` cases whose programs read their own argv (see `ArgsMarker`).
//
// MULTI-FILE TESTS — `// --- file: <name>` (added for the cross-file signature rung).
//
// ⚠ **THIS CONVENTION IS PORTED, NOT INVENTED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** It already exists
// and the corpus already uses it: **650 occurrences across 236 files** in `/specs` and its
// committed fragments, implemented by the C# reference in `SpecParser.cs`'s
// `SplitMultiFileSource()` + `FileMarkerRegex()`. shv2 simply did not read it. A second,
// shv2-only multi-file syntax would be this project's signature bug — one fact written down twice
// — in the one place it would be hardest to notice, because both would "work".
//
// The rules, mirrored exactly (`SpecParser.cs:273`):
//   • the marker is a COMMENT LINE inside the ```maxon block: `// --- file: app/main.maxon`;
//   • a block with NO marker is a single-file test (this is the common case, and it is unchanged);
//   • a file's text runs from its marker to the next one (or the end), TRIMMED;
//   • anything before the FIRST marker is discarded;
//   • forward slashes make subdirectories — and a `.` or `..` segment is REJECTED, because these
//     names are written to a temp directory and `..` walks out of it (the reference rejects it at
//     parse; shv2 rejects it at the WRITE, which is the point where the escape would actually
//     happen — see SpecTestRunner.writeSourceFiles).
//
// The golden `.test` fragment still shows the MERGED block, markers and all — again the
// reference's behaviour — so what a reviewer reads is what a spec author wrote.
//
// DEFERRED-SKIP CONVENTION (why a `## Deferred` section, not a comment):
// HTML comments do NOT nest. The obvious way to shelve a test —
// `<!-- … <!-- test: NAME --> … -->` — is a trap: the FIRST `-->` (the one on
// the inner `<!-- test: … -->` line) closes the OUTER comment, so the inner test
// marker + its code blocks are exposed as live markup and a naive parser
// extracts and RUNS a deferred test. Rather than depend on comment nesting, the
// active-test region runs from the `## Tests` heading to the next STOP HEADING
// (see `RegionEndHeadings`) or EOF. Deferred tests live under a `## Deferred`
// heading (as plain `### name` subsections, carrying NO `<!-- test: … -->`
// marker), so they are both out of the scanned region AND carry no marker —
// belt and suspenders. The specs-shv2 files were rewritten to this convention
// in lockstep with this parser.
//
// DISABLED TESTS — the per-CASE shelf, and the mechanism Workstream S runs on.
// `## Deferred` shelves at the wrong granularity for a ported spec: a real
// `/specs` file mixes cases shv2 can already pass with cases it cannot, so
// deferring the FILE strands the former behind the latter. A case shv2 cannot
// yet pass is therefore marked `<!-- disabled-test: NAME -->` — the marker the
// project ALREADY has (v1 skips it at SpecTestRunner.maxon:2233; the C# runner's
// regex is `<!--\s*(?:disabled-)?test:\s*\S+\s*-->`) — with the RUNG THAT
// UNLOCKS IT on the following comment line:
//
//     <!-- disabled-test: arithmetic.string-concat -->
//     <!-- P1.2 String -->
//
// A disabled case is parsed as a full test BOUNDARY (its blocks are consumed
// exactly as an active test's are, by the same routine, so the two can never
// drift on where a test ends) and then discarded: never compiled, never run, and
// — because it never reaches `SpecFile.tests` — NO `.test` golden is generated
// for it. Goldens therefore accrete only as cases are ENABLED, which is what
// keeps the fragment diff reviewable instead of a ten-thousand-file dump.
//
// The reasons are the roadmap: `grep -A1 disabled-test:` groups the entire
// remaining language surface by the milestone that unlocks it, ranked by cases
// that must actually PASS. And the ratchet is free: an ENABLED case may never be
// re-disabled.

// --- Spec-markdown grammar tokens ---
// Named (not inline literals) because these strings are a format contract shared
// with spec authors, and because the ORDER two of them are matched in is
// load-bearing: "```maxoncstderr" startsWith "```maxon", so the compiler-error
// fence MUST be tested before the source fence or the source check swallows it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE FENCE PREFIX RELATIONS, IN ONE PLACE — because a swallowed arm is SILENT.**
// `scanTestFromMarker` recognizes every fence with `startsWith`, so if fence A is a prefix of fence B
// and A's arm is matched first, B's blocks are read as A's and B is never seen. This set contains
// exactly ONE such pair (the one named above), and it is handled by ORDER; every other pair must be in
// no prefix relation at all, and each is checked here rather than assumed — fence order is precisely the
// kind of thing that stays true until someone edits a literal. (`String.startsWith` compares BYTES, so a
// difference of CASE is a real difference. Byte 3 is the first character after the ``` opener.)
//
//   • **PORTABLE vs PORTABLE.** "```maxoncstderr" startsWith "```maxon" — THE pair, ordered. Every other
//     pair differs at byte 3 (`m`/`e`/`s` — both `maxon` spellings against "```exitcode", "```stderr" and
//     "```stdout"), except "```stderr" vs "```stdout", which first differ at byte 6 (`e`/`o`).
//   • **QUALIFIED vs PORTABLE — separated BY CONSTRUCTION, not by enumeration.** A target-qualified fence
//     (`` ```Stdout:<target> `` and its twins) is ``` plus the portable name with its first letter
//     CAPITALIZED plus `:`, so byte 3 is an UPPERCASE letter in every qualified fence and a LOWERCASE one
//     in every portable fence. Two strings differing at byte 3 are in no prefix relation either way — so
//     this holds for a portable fence added LATER too, which an enumeration could not promise.
//   • **QUALIFIED vs QUALIFIED.** "```Maxoncstderr:" differs from the other two at byte 3 (`M` vs `S`);
//     "```Stdout:" and "```Stderr:" SHARE the six bytes "```Std" and first differ at byte 6 (`o`/`e`) —
//     the same distance apart as their lowercase originals, one case-flip up. Sharing a prefix is
//     harmless — neither is a prefix OF the other — but it does mean neither may ever be recognized by a
//     test that stops short of byte 6, which is the shortcut this one pair invites.
//   • **THE TWO CAPTURE FENCES.** "```mm-trace" is in no prefix relation with any other: it differs from
//     both `maxon` spellings at byte 4 (`m`/`a` — they share the `m` at byte 3 and nothing further),
//     from "```exitcode", "```stderr" and "```stdout" at byte 3 (`m` vs `e`/`s`), and from every
//     capitalized fence at byte 3 by the QUALIFIED-vs-PORTABLE rule two bullets up (its byte 3 is
//     lowercase). Nothing is a prefix of it and it is a prefix of nothing, so its arm's position is free.
//     "```log-trace" is separated from it at byte 3 (`l` vs `m`), from `maxon` at byte 3 as well, and
//     from "```exitcode"/"```stderr"/"```stdout" there too — so the SECOND capture fence joined without
//     disturbing the relation this bullet states, which is the check every added fence owes.
//   • **THE `Required*` FAMILY**, which is the one group the capitalization rule does not separate from
//     the qualified fences, because it is capitalized at byte 3 as well: "```RequiredData",
//     "```RequiredRdata" and "```RequiredRuntime" all differ from "```Maxoncstderr:", "```Stderr:" and
//     "```Stdout:" at byte 3 (`R` vs `M`/`S`), and from each other at byte 11 (`D`/`R`) and byte 12
//     (`d`/`u`).
//
// ⇒ no arm can swallow another, and the order every arm but the compiler-error/source pair is matched in
// stays free. Each fence constant below names what it PINS; none of them restates this relation.
let FrontmatterFence = "---"
let StatusKey = "status:"
let TestsHeading = "## Tests"
export let SectionHeadingPrefix = "## "
export let TestMarker = "<!-- test:"

// The ONE heading that ENDS the active-test region. Every OTHER `## ` heading is an
// ordinary section heading INSIDE `## Tests`, and the region continues across it:
// `/specs/array-sort.md` organizes its 40 cases under six `## Stage N: …` headings, and a
// rule that stopped at ANY heading ran the 12 cases above `## Stage 2`, dropped the other
// 28, and reported the file green. Neither reference parser has such a rule — the C#
// reference (`SpecParser.cs`) keys on `## Documentation` / `## Tests` only, and v1 keys on
// `## Tests` / `## Documentation` — so this stop is shv2's, and it is narrow.
//
// ⚠ It cannot be replaced by "a heading with no test markers under it": the `## Deferred`
// section's own PROSE quotes the marker literally ("prefix it with its `<!-- test: NAME -->`
// marker"), so a marker-driven scan would read that sentence as a case. The stop is
// POSITIONAL, and the headings that qualify are named here — the one place a future stop
// heading joins. Matched with `startsWith`, so a decorated stop heading
// (`## Deferred (P1.6)`) still stops rather than silently exposing the section below it.
let RegionEndHeadings = ["## Deferred"]

// A case shv2 cannot yet pass — same grammar, opposite verdict. It is NOT a
// superstring of `TestMarker` ("<!-- disabled-test:" does not contain
// "<!-- test:"), so the two match orders are independent and neither can swallow
// the other. Both are still tested explicitly, in one place, so that stays true
// if either literal is ever edited.
export let DisabledTestMarker = "<!-- disabled-test:"

// The per-target gate — a marker that may sit on any comment line between a test's
// `<!-- test: NAME -->` (or `<!-- disabled-test: NAME -->`) and its first ```maxon block. It restricts
// the test to the `targetToString` forms it names (`x64-windows`, `arm64-macos`); on any other target
// the runner SKIPS it. A missing marker — or one with a blank value — is "no restriction": the test
// runs on every target (see `SpecTest.targets` and `SpecTestRunner.markerAllowsTarget`).
//
// It is NOT a superstring of `TestMarker` or `DisabledTestMarker` ("<!-- targets:" first differs from
// "<!-- test:" at the `a`/`e`), so the marker checks are independent and none can swallow another —
// exactly the property that keeps `DisabledTestMarker` and `TestMarker` from colliding.
//
// COMMA is the separator, not because shv2 chose it but because the spec format is SHARED: the C#
// reference (`SpecParser.cs` `TargetsDirectiveRegex`) and v1 (`SpecParser.maxon`
// `parseTargetsDirective`) both split a `targets:` value on `,`, and these are the same `.md` files a
// third compiler reads. A second, shv2-only spelling of one directive would be this project's
// signature bug — one fact written down twice — in a place where both would "work".
let TargetsMarker = "<!-- targets:"
let TargetSeparator = ","

// The per-case RUNTIME ARGUMENTS — `<!-- Args: -5.5 -->` — sitting on the same kind of comment line
// `TargetsMarker` does, between a test's marker and its first fence. Its payload is the argv the
// compiled program is SPAWNED with (`SpecTest.programArgs` → `SpecTestRunner.buildRunCommand`), which
// the program then reads back through `CommandLine.args()`.
//
// `/specs` carries 51 of them across 12 files (`abs`, `ceil`/`floor`, `min`/`max`, `command-line-args`,
// `regalloc`, `advent`, `builtins-type`), so a harness that spawns an empty argv cannot port any of
// them: the program reads `args.get(1)`, gets nothing, and the case fails on a value it was never
// given. Two already-ported specs record exactly that in their shelve notes.
//
// It is not a superstring of any other marker ("<!-- Args:" first differs from "<!-- test:",
// "<!-- disabled-test:" and "<!-- targets:" at the character after "<!-- "), so the marker checks stay
// independent and none can swallow another — the property `TargetsMarker` and `DisabledTestMarker` are
// both documented against.
//
// ⚠ CAPITAL `A`, matched EXACTLY, because the spec format is SHARED: the C# reference's
// `ArgsDirectiveRegex` is `<!--\s*Args:\s*(.+?)\s*-->` and these are the same `.md` files it reads. A
// case-insensitive shv2 spelling would accept — and act on — a directive the reference silently ignores,
// which is the one-file-two-meanings failure this file's other shared constants exist to prevent.
//
// ⚠ **IT DECIDES NOTHING ABOUT WHICH TARGETS THE CASE RUNS ON, AND IT USED TO.** The only route from a
// Maxon program to argv is the `__cl_*` runtime, which is x64-windows only at this rung, so a case
// carrying this marker cannot pass anywhere else — and the runner used to read that restriction off
// THIS marker rather than making the spec author write a second `<!-- targets: x64-windows -->` beside
// it. The placement argument was right and survives: argv is the harness's DELIVERY MECHANISM, never
// the subject of `abs`/`max`/`trunc`, so a per-case marker would state a limitation of the mechanism as
// a property of the maths. What was wrong was the direction of the implication (PLAN row `X2`): the
// marker is SUFFICIENT for "this case needs argv" and NOT NECESSARY — `command-line-args
// .args-length-no-extra` reads argv with no marker at all, because the property it pins is that argv[0]
// arrives ALONE. The restriction is now read off the COMPILER's own E3104 refusal, which needs no
// marker of any kind; see `SpecTestRunner.targetRefusalIn`.
let ArgsMarker = "<!-- Args:"

// ⭐ **THE ONE VALUELESS MARKER — `<!-- AsyncTrace -->`.** It says a case asserts the green-thread TRACE, which
// changes two things and neither is a value: the program is compiled with `--async-trace`, and both sides of
// the stderr comparison are put through `normalizeAsyncTraceStderr` first.
//
// ⚠ **IT CARRIES NO PAYLOAD, SO IT MUST NOT GO THROUGH `markerValue`.** That helper splits on
// `MarkerNameSep` and answers "" for a bare marker — an empty answer being indistinguishable from a
// missing one is exactly how a silently-ignored marker happens. Its arm is a pure `contains` flag set.
//
// ⛔ **AND ONE WAS ALREADY BEING IGNORED.** `specs-shv2/async-await.md` has carried `<!-- AsyncTrace -->` since
// the port, with no arm to claim it: an unrecognized comment line falls through this scanner's `i = i + 1` and
// is DISCARDED without complaint, so the case ran as an ordinary stderr assertion and simply failed. A marker
// nothing reads is not inert — it is a claim the file makes and the harness silently declines.
//
// It is not a superstring or substring of any other marker ("<!-- AsyncTrace" first differs from "<!-- test:",
// "<!-- disabled-test:", "<!-- targets:" and "<!-- Args:" at the character after "<!-- "), so the marker checks
// stay independent and none can swallow another.
let AsyncTraceMarker = "<!-- AsyncTrace"

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH FAMILY OF THE DEBUG STREAM A CAPTURE CASE ASSERTS.** Both families ride the SAME shared-memory
// ring, are built the same way (`--debugstream`) and are read by the same consumer (`maxon monitor`), and
// they differ in exactly four derived things: the marker that requests one, the fence that holds the
// golden, the `--filter=` the monitor is given, and the prefix a decoded line of that family starts with.
// That is why this is ONE mode with a family rather than two capture modes — a second copy of the build
// flag, the target restriction, the normalizer, the remint and the splice is five places for the two to
// drift.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO ASK DIFFERENT QUESTIONS OF THE SAME PROGRAM.** `mm` is the RUNTIME talking about itself —
// what the program allocated, retained and freed. `log` is the PROGRAM's own sentences, written through
// the `__DebugStream` builtin. A case selects one; asking for both at once would need a golden whose lines
// interleave two families, which no reader has wanted.
export enum TraceFamily
	// The memory manager's own events — `mm_alloc` / `mm_incref` / `mm_decref` / `mm_free`.
	mm
	// The events the program AUTHORS through `__DebugStream` — `log_phase_begin` / `log_event` /
	// `log_text` / `log_phase_end`.
	log
end 'TraceFamily'

// ⭐ **THE SECOND AND THIRD VALUELESS MARKERS — `<!-- MmTrace -->` and `<!-- LogTrace -->`.** Each says a
// case asserts one family of the debug stream: `MmTrace` the allocations it makes, the refcount
// transitions they go through and the frees that end them; `LogTrace` the events its own source writes.
// Like `<!-- AsyncTrace -->` they change how the program is BUILT (`--debugstream`, which emits the
// DebugStream producer) and how its output is COMPARED (the run happens under `maxon monitor --filter=…`
// and both sides go through `normalizeTrace`), and neither is a value.
//
// ⚠ **THE MODE IS ENTERED BY EITHER THE MARKER OR THE BLOCK**, which is the reference's rule
// (`TestRunner.cs`, `fragment.MmTrace`) and the one the spec's own prose states. The marker alone is the
// AUTHORING state: write it, run `--update-required`, and the harness mints the matching block from what
// the program actually did. So a marker with no block is not an error — it is a request.
//
// ⚠ Neither carries a payload, so neither may go through `markerValue` — see `AsyncTraceMarker` for why an
// empty answer being indistinguishable from a missing one is the failure that arrangement prevents.
//
// Neither is a superstring or substring of any other marker ("<!-- MmTrace" and "<!-- LogTrace" first
// differ from every one of them, and from each other, at the character after "<!-- "), so the marker checks
// stay independent and none can swallow another.
let MmTraceMarker = "<!-- MmTrace"
let LogTraceMarker = "<!-- LogTrace"

// The marker a family's case is REQUESTED with. Read by the splice, which has to find the line it inserts
// a minted golden after — see `SpecTestRunner.traceMarkerLineOf`.
export function traceMarkerOf(family TraceFamily) returns String
	return match family 'f'
		mm gives MmTraceMarker
		log gives LogTraceMarker
	end 'f'
end 'traceMarkerOf'

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS CASE NEEDS FROM THE NETWORK — `<!-- network: live -->`, the marker that keeps somebody
// else's uptime out of this suite's verdict.** The cases carrying it across `tcp-client`, `async-tcp` and
// `http-client` open a socket to a REAL EXTERNAL HOST (`tcpbin.com`, `httpbin.org`). A run of those is not
// a claim about this compiler: it is green when the public internet is up and red when it is not, and the
// difference between the two is invisible in the report. So a case carrying this marker is left OUT of a
// default run and comes back with `--network` (see `SpecTestRunner.networkModeAllows`).
//
// ⚠ **IT IS PER-CASE, AND THE SPEC-LEVEL SPELLING CANNOT DO THIS JOB.** Both reference runners gate whole
// SPECS on a `category: network` frontmatter key, and all three of these specs also hold DETERMINISTIC
// error-path cases (`connect-error`, `resolve-error`, `invalid-url`, `build-request`) that exercise the
// real networking runtime without leaving the machine and must keep running by default. Gating the file
// would take those down with the live ones.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS NOT `disabled-test:`.** A shelved case is one shv2 cannot pass; these pass, and are
// PARSED, SELECTED and RUN in full under `--network`. The two must not read alike.
//
// ⚠ **NEW VOCABULARY, DELIBERATELY, AND THE REFERENCES IGNORE IT SILENTLY — WHICH IS THE CORRECT
// BEHAVIOUR FOR THEM.** v1 hard-codes seven paths in its own runner
// (`SpecTestRunner.liveNetworkFragments`), which is the spec files' contents written down a second time in
// a compiler; the copy that rots is always the one in the runner, and it rots SILENTLY because a path that
// no longer names a case excludes nothing.
//
// ⇒ **AND IT HAS ALREADY ROTTED — MEASURED 2026-08-21.** v1's seven do not include
// `http-client/http-client.async-trace-interleave`, which is live and would reach `httpbin.org` on v1 too.
// Worse, the number was TRANSCRIBED from that roster into six places in THIS harness, so an argument against
// hand-written rosters shipped with a hand-written count. Both are gone; the marker is the census.
// ⛔ Do not write the number down again, here or anywhere.
//
// Stating the fact on the case itself is the same correction the section-pin markers got (see
// `SpecTestRunner.targetCanCheckSectionPins`). The C# reference and v1 skip
// an unrecognized `<!-- … -->` comment line, so these `.md` files keep exactly the meaning they have there
// today — v1's hard-coded list still excludes them, the bootstrap still runs them.
//
// It is not a superstring or substring of any other marker ("<!-- network:" first differs from
// "<!-- test:", "<!-- disabled-test:", "<!-- targets:", "<!-- Args:", "<!-- AsyncTrace", "<!-- MmTrace"
// and "<!-- LogTrace"
// at the character after "<!-- "), so the marker checks stay independent and none can swallow another.
export let NetworkMarker = "<!-- network:"

// The ONE value `NetworkMarker` recognizes. Any other value is REFUSED rather than read as "no
// restriction": a marker the harness silently declines is a claim the spec makes and nothing honours,
// which is the failure `AsyncTraceMarker` documents having actually shipped.
export let NetworkLiveValue = "live"

// What separates two arguments in an `<!-- Args: … -->` payload, and what quotes one that must survive
// the split intact. See `parseProgramArgs` for why the quote is not decoration.
let ArgSeparator = " "
let ArgQuote = "\""

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A MARKER LINE IS — THE REFERENCE'S ANSWER, AND SHV2 HAD A NARROWER ONE AT BOTH ENDS.**
//
// The constants above are the CANONICAL SPELLING a spec author writes and `traceMarkerLineOf` looks
// for. They are not what is MATCHED, and matching them literally was two silent divergences from the
// oracle at once — measured 2026-08-24, neither live in either corpus, which is exactly how the one
// that WAS live (`negate-float`) got in:
//
//   • `<!--  test: x -->` (two spaces) never contained the literal `<!-- test:`, so shv2 saw NO marker
//     at all and walked the line over as prose — the case silently DROPPED, its fences swallowed by the
//     case above it — while `<!--\s*test:` matched and the bootstrap RAN it.
//   • `<!-- test: x<TAB>-->` never contained the literal `" -->"`, so once this rung's unterminated
//     refusal landed it took the WHOLE SUITE DOWN on every run, while `\s*-->` accepted it.
//
// ⇒ the three tokens below are what the scanner matches, and the whitespace rule around them lives in
// `markerOpeningsIn` / `markerValue` rather than in a literal per marker. EVERY marker in this grammar
// goes through them, so closing this class closed it for `targets:`, `Args:`, `network:` and the three
// valueless flags at the same time — each had the identical divergence and none was named as a defect,
// because nothing had asked.
let CommentOpen = "<!--"
let MarkerEnd = "-->"
let MarkerNameSep = ":"
let SourceFence = "```maxon"
let ExitCodeFence = "```exitcode"
let CompilerErrorFence = "```maxoncstderr"

// The RUN's stderr — what the COMPILED PROGRAM printed, not what the compiler said about
// it. That distinction is the whole reason this is a separate fence from
// `CompilerErrorFence` and not a spelling of it: `maxoncstderr` asserts the compile FAILED
// and pins the diagnostic, while this asserts the compile SUCCEEDED, the program RAN, and
// pins what it wrote — the panic path. 32 blocks in `/specs` use it.
let RuntimeStderrFence = "```stderr"

// The RUN's stdout — what the COMPILED PROGRAM printed to standard output (P1.2 wave A — `print`). A
// separate fence from `RuntimeStderrFence` for the same reason that one is separate from
// `CompilerErrorFence`: it pins a DIFFERENT stream. Until this rung shv2 could `print` nothing, so a
// ```stdout block was silently walked over — a false green the moment `print` landed.
let StdoutFence = "```stdout"

// ⭐ **THE PER-TARGET EXPECTED STDOUT — `` ```Stdout:x64-windows ``, and the fence a PORTED spec needs
// when one program prints different bytes on different targets.** `specs/filepath.md` is the canonical
// case and the reason this arrived: a path separator is `\` on Windows and `/` everywhere else, so
// **26 of its 40 cases carry BOTH** a `` ```Stdout:x64-windows `` block and a portable ```stdout one.
// Without this fence shv2 read the portable block on Windows and failed every one of them with a
// mismatch whose "actual" was the CORRECT answer — a red that says nothing about the compiler.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESOLUTION IS THE REFERENCE'S, NOT A NEW ONE** (`SpecParser.cs:177-185`): a block naming THIS
// run's target WINS over the portable ```stdout block, and a block naming any other target is consumed
// and discarded. Order in the file is irrelevant — the reference matches each fence independently, so a
// spec author may write either block first, and `specs/filepath.md` writes the qualified one first.
// A second, shv2-only resolution rule would be this project's signature bug in a file THREE compilers'
// suites read.
let TargetStdoutFencePrefix = "```Stdout:"

// ⭐⭐ **THE OTHER TWO TARGET-QUALIFIED FENCES — `` ```Maxoncstderr:<target> `` and `` ```Stderr:<target> ``,
// the COMPILE-ERROR and RUNTIME-STDERR twins of the block above.** They arrived the moment a DIAGNOSTIC'S
// OWN TEXT became target-dependent: `ExitCode`'s range is `int(0 to u32.max)` on Windows and
// `int(0 to 255)` on Linux, macOS and WASI (`stdlib/Process.maxon`), so E3005's "outside the range of
// 'ExitCode' (…)" prints different bytes per target — and `specs-shv2/exit-code-range.md`'s rejected cases
// RUN on the wasm lane, so one portable block cannot state their expectation. Before this, `` ```Stdout: ``
// was the only per-target fence: a spec could say a PROGRAM printed different bytes per target but not
// that the COMPILER did.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESOLUTION IS `TargetStdoutFencePrefix`'S, NOT A SECOND AND A THIRD ONE** — a block naming THIS
// run's target WINS over the portable block, a block naming any other target is consumed and discarded,
// and order in the file is irrelevant. All three go through `TargetPreferredText` and
// `recordTargetQualifiedText`, so the rule exists once rather than once per slot.
//
// ⚠ **THE SPELLING EXTENDS THE REFERENCE'S CONVENTION RATHER THAN INVENTING ONE.** The C# parser
// qualifies a fence by CAPITALIZING its name and appending `:<targetKey>` — `Stdout:` and `RequiredIR:`
// (`SpecParser.cs:190`, `:197`). These are those same two moves applied to `maxoncstderr` and `stderr`. The
// reference implements neither yet, and its `KnownCodeBlockPrefixes` would have to learn them before a
// spec carrying one could move into `/specs`; spelling them the way it WOULD spell them is what keeps
// that a one-line addition instead of a second syntax for one idea.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`` ```Stderr: `` IS MATCHED BY NO SPEC TODAY, AND THAT IS STATED HERE RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE
// DISCOVERED (BATCH27 review).** Only `` ```Maxoncstderr: `` had a case that needed it; the run's stderr
// has so far been split per target by writing two cases with `<!-- targets: … -->` instead
// (`exit-code-range.md`'s `computed-argument-is-guarded-at-the-callee-entry` and its wasm twin, which the
// spec argues must stay two cases because they pin different facts). **So the LITERAL below is the one
// byte-sequence in this file no run exercises**, and the argument that it is nonetheless not the same
// claim as the fence refused just below is exactly one thing: it is a THIRD INSTANCE of a mechanism whose
// other two instances run on every spec in the suite. `TargetPreferredText` already holds the `stderr`
// slot, `recordTargetQualifiedText` already compares the suffix, and both are exercised in both
// directions by `` ```Stdout: `` and `` ```Maxoncstderr: `` — so the only property unique to this constant
// is that it is in no prefix relation with another fence, which is DERIVED in the grammar-token header
// rather than observed by a run. An asymmetric set would be the worse trade: a `stderr` slot that can
// never carry a qualified block is a hole in `TargetPreferredText` shaped like the second resolution rule
// this type exists to prevent.
//
// ⭐ **AND THERE IS DELIBERATELY NO `` ```Exitcode:<target> ``, WHICH IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF ABSENCE.**
// Nothing needs one — and unlike the fence above it would not be a third instance of anything: an exit
// code is a `SpecExitCode`, not a String, so it would need a FOURTH slot type with its own pair of
// blocks, its own preference and its own resolution, none of which any spec would run. An unused
// mechanism is a claim nobody checks, and that is a claim with new code under it.
//
// ⚠⚠ Adding two fences to a set that ALREADY CONTAINS A SWALLOWING PAIR is exactly where a second such
// pair would appear unnoticed. Both prefixes are therefore argued against every other fence in the ONE
// place that whole relation is stated — the `--- Spec-markdown grammar tokens ---` header, which is also
// where the pair that DOES swallow is named. Nothing here restates it.
let TargetCompilerErrorFencePrefix = "```Maxoncstderr:"
let TargetStderrFencePrefix = "```Stderr:"

// ⭐ **THE DECODED EVENT TRACE OF ONE FAMILY — what `maxon monitor --filter=…` printed, normalized.**
// One event per line, in emission order, with the monitor's timestamp prefix and depth indent stripped and
// the family's own volatile fields reduced (see `SpecTestRunner.normalizeTrace` for why every one of those
// steps is needed to make the text stable across runs and machines):
//   • ```mm-trace  — `mm_<verb> <Tag> #<id>[ size=N | rc=N]`, allocation ids densely renumbered from 1.
//   • ```log-trace — `log_<verb> [<name>] [cat=N lvl=N] unit=N […]`, with the emitting green thread and
//     processor dropped.
//
// ⚠ **EITHER IS ITSELF A RESULT CHECK.** A case carrying one needs no ```exitcode / ```stdout / ```stderr
// block to be a test — it is asserting what the program did rather than what it computed, which no other
// block can state — so `pinsAnyResult` counts it.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE TWO FENCES ARE IN NO PREFIX RELATION WITH EACH OTHER OR WITH ANY OTHER FENCE.** "```log-trace"
// first differs from "```mm-trace" at the character after the backticks, which is the relation the
// `--- Spec-markdown grammar tokens ---` header requires of every added pair.
export let MmTraceFence = "```mm-trace"
export let LogTraceFence = "```log-trace"

// The fence a family's golden lives in. Read by the parser (which claims the block) and by the splice
// (which writes one), so the two cannot come to disagree about which fence holds which family.
export function traceFenceOf(family TraceFamily) returns String
	return match family 'f'
		mm gives MmTraceFence
		log gives LogTraceFence
	end 'f'
end 'traceFenceOf'

// ⭐ A LINKED SECTION'S BYTES, as typed values — `i64 42` / `i8 1` / `f64 3.14`, one per line. Not
// what the compiler thinks it laid out, but what the linked binary actually contains, read back out
// of the image (`SpecTestRunner.checkRequiredSections`).
//
// ⚠ **```RequiredData WAS SILENTLY IGNORED UNTIL P1.0d.5b, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS HERE.** This parser
// dropped every one on the floor — the outer scan, finding no marker in it, walked over it line by
// line — so the SIX cases in `specs/static-variables.md` that carry one would have passed on their
// exit code alone while CLAIMING to check their layout. A gate that cannot see its own property must
// not report PASS; that is this project's own rule, and `verify-warm-rebuild` had just been caught
// breaking it. Implemented rather than refused, because the reference already implements it
// (`TestRunner.cs:CheckRequiredData` → `CheckRequiredSection`) and porting it costs less than
// explaining why shv2 would not.
//
// ⭐⭐ **```RequiredRdata IS ITS TWIN, AND IT CLOSES THE SAME BLINDNESS ONE SECTION OVER.** A
// `.rdata` payload registered under a STRUCTURAL label (`registerRdataConstantLabeled` — witness
// tables, `__layout_*` descriptors, the UCD tables) does not advance `GlobalDataTable.nextStringId`,
// so it shifts read-only OFFSETS without moving any label a golden fragment prints: the fragment
// shows which label a `lea` names and nothing shows what is IN it. The name is not an invention —
// this file's header lists `RequiredRdata` among the v1 features dropped until "a spec first needs
// it", and BATCH8 is that moment.
//
// The two are ONE mechanism taking a `LinkedSectionKind` (see `LinkedSection.maxon`): one fence
// table, one block parse, one prefix compare, one message, two section constants.
//
// ⛔⛔ **WRITING A ```RequiredRdata BLOCK? THE PIN IS A PREFIX FROM BYTE 0 OF THE SECTION, SO YOUR BLOB HAS
// TO BE THE FIRST PAYLOAD THE PROGRAM REGISTERS.** There is no label lookup — `.rdata` is a concatenation in
// registration order — so a case pinning it must contain **no string literal, no `print`, and no call into a
// generic body**, because a string blob (plus its 56-byte record), a witness table and a `__layout_*`
// descriptor all register during the same lowering walk, in op order, ahead of anything below them. A panic
// message registers later (`insertRangeChecks` runs after lowering) and is therefore safe.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS A PROPERTY OF THE PROGRAM YOU WRITE, HELD BY NOTHING.** Add a `print` above the array literal
// in such a case and the pin fails with a byte mismatch pointing at YOUR blob rather than at the string that
// displaced it. `SpecTestRunner.compareLinkedSection` carries the mechanism and why the label-anchored read
// that would fix it is a rung of its own.
let RequiredDataFence = "```RequiredData"
let RequiredRdataFence = "```RequiredRdata"

// ⭐⭐ **THE EMITTED RUNTIME FUNCTIONS THIS TEST'S GOLDEN RENDERS — one `__`/`mrt_`-prefixed name per
// line, and the reason is that a compiler EMITS more than a program DECLARES.**
//
// A golden fragment shows only functions parsed from source (`TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction`), so
// the entry stub, the allocator, and the whole `__str_*`/`__managed_*` family carry NO golden at all —
// the one gate that could see them renders none of them, and the miss is measured rather than
// feared: `__str_bytes_view`'s owed-base arm holds an incref whose loss is a use-after-free that
// BALANCES (the matching release goes with it), so the exit code, the leak gate and every fragment
// stayed green over it together.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS OPT-IN PER TEST, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN.** Rendering the runtime everywhere would
// inject thousands of identical lines into every one of the corpus's goldens and move all of them at
// once on any runtime change; a test without this block renders byte-identically to one written
// before the block existed.
//
// ⚠ It is NOT a result block: it says nothing about the RUN, so it does not satisfy
// `pinsAnyResult` — a case carrying only this would compile, render a golden and check no behaviour.
let RequiredRuntimeFence = "```RequiredRuntime"

// The three-backtick delimiter. Markdown spells OPEN and CLOSE the same way, so one name serves both
// readers: `readFence` looking for the end of a block, and the terminating arm in `scanTestFromMarker`
// looking at the start of one it has no reader for.
export let FenceDelimiter = "```"

// The one TAGGED fence a test region may open without claiming anything. A bare `FenceDelimiter` block
// and a ```text one are PROSE — a quoted panic message, an illustrated diagnostic — and 200 of them are
// in this corpus. Every OTHER tag is a claim, and `isUnimplementedFenceOpen` refuses the ones no arm of
// `scanTestFromMarker` can honour.
let ProseFenceOpen = "```text"

// The multi-file marker, and the ONE line the `// Test: <name>` fragment header adds above a
// block's first line. The header is why a file's line 1 is not the block's line 1 — see
// `SpecSourceFile.mergedLineOffset`, and `SpecTestRunner.fragmentSource`, which writes it.
export let FileMarker = "// --- file:"
let FragmentHeaderLines = 1 as LineIndex

// ⭐⭐ **THE STDLIB-OVERLAY MARKER — how a case reaches a rule that only `<stdlibDir>/Builtins.maxon`
// can trip.** Its value is a path RELATIVE TO `stdlib/`, and the section's text is written at the TOP
// of the runner's private copy of that file (`SpecTestRunner.stageStdlibOverlay`, which states the
// isolation argument and why the position is a contract rather than a detail).
//
// It exists because three of `reserved-double-underscore.md`'s own findings were recorded as SHELL
// TRANSCRIPTS: every route to E4015 through `FunctionNameIndex` needs a declaration carrying the
// reserved `__` prefix, exactly one FILE may write one, and a spec fragment can never be that file.
// A finding a suite cannot re-run is a check that cannot fail.
export let StdlibOverlayMarker = "// --- stdlib-overlay:"

// The prefix BOTH section markers share, and therefore the shape a MISSPELLED one has. A line that
// opens with it and matches neither marker is refused rather than kept as source — without that,
// `// --- stblib-overlay: Builtins.maxon` is just a comment, the overlay silently never happens, and
// the case reports PASS for a program that was never staged.
//
// It is a third literal rather than a slice of `FileMarker`, because slicing needs a hardcoded length
// — a magic number standing in for the very prefix it was meant to stop repeating. What keeps the
// three in step is a CHECK, not a derivation: `splitSourceSections` refuses to run if either marker
// stops starting with it.
let SectionMarkerPrefix = "// --- "

// What would let a `// --- file:` marker escape the temp directory it is written into: a `.`/`..`
// segment, a rooted name, or a Windows drive letter. Checked at the write
// (SpecTestRunner.isSafeRelativeName), named here beside the marker they qualify.
export let DotSegment = "."
export let ParentSegment = ".."
export let PathSeparator = "/"
export let WindowsPathSeparator = "\\"
export let DriveLetterSeparator = ":"

// A 0-based line index into a spec file — and, on `SpecSourceFile.mergedLineOffset`, the distance
// between a compiled file's line numbers and the merged fragment's. Exported because the RUNNER is
// what applies that distance to a diagnostic.
export typealias LineIndex = int(0 to u64.max)

// How many cases in one spec file are shelved behind `<!-- disabled-test: … -->`.
// Carried (not discarded) because it is the project's progress metric: a rung is
// DONE when the cases it unlocks have moved out of this count and into the run.
export typealias DisabledTestCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// A process exit code — shared by the EXPECTED value parsed here and the OBSERVED
// value the runner reads back, so they compare without a cast. Exported so the
// runner names the `SpecExpectation.exitCode` payload in its own signatures.
export typealias ExitCodeValue = int(i64.min to i64.max)

// What a spec pins about the RUN's stderr. A UNION, not an empty `String`: "this spec
// says nothing about stderr" and "this spec says stderr is EMPTY" are different claims,
// and an empty String cannot tell them apart — it would be exactly the sentinel the
// project forbids. Most tests pin nothing; the panic specs pin the diagnostic.
export union SpecStderr
	unpinned
	pinned(text String)
end 'SpecStderr'

// What a spec pins about the RUN's stdout — `SpecStderr`'s exact shape, and a union for the identical
// reason: "this spec says nothing about stdout" and "this spec says stdout is EMPTY" are different
// claims, and an empty String cannot tell them apart. `unpinned` is NOT "ignore stdout" — it asserts
// the program printed nothing (see `checkRunStdout`), which is what a non-`print` program should do.
export union SpecStdout
	unpinned
	pinned(text String)
end 'SpecStdout'

// ONE ```RequiredData / ```RequiredRdata block: which section it names, and the typed-value text it
// claims for it.
//
// ⚠ **AN ARRAY OF THESE REPLACED A `SpecStderr`-SHAPED UNION.** `unpinned`/`pinned` existed to
// distinguish "says nothing about `.data`" from "says `.data` is EMPTY", which an empty String
// cannot do; an ARRAY draws the first half of that distinction with no arm at all — a case that says
// nothing contributes no ENTRY — and unlike a pair of union-valued fields it does not have to grow a
// field per section. The count is the literal number of blocks the case wrote, so it is not a
// sentinel.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE SECOND HALF OF THAT DISTINCTION DOES NOT EXIST, AND THE UNION NEVER DELIVERED IT EITHER.**
// "The section is EMPTY" is not a claim this gate can make, because the compare is a PREFIX
// (`SpecTestRunner.compareLinkedSection`) and the empty prefix matches every image ever linked —
// `pinned("")` was as vacuous as `pinned` was expressive. An empty block is therefore REFUSED at
// parse time (`refuseVacuousDirective`) rather than recorded as a claim nothing can fail.
export type SpecSectionPin
	export let section as LinkedSectionKind
	export let text as String

	export static function create(section LinkedSectionKind, text String) returns SpecSectionPin
		return Self{section: section, text: text}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecSectionPin'

export typealias SpecSectionPinArray = Array with SpecSectionPin

// (The emitted-runtime names a ```RequiredRuntime block lists are carried in
// `RuntimeFunctionNameArray`, declared by the printer that consumes them — `TargetPrinter.maxon`.
// It is the same list all the way from the fence to the render, so it is named once.)

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT A SPEC PINS ABOUT THE EXIT CODE — AND UNTIL BATCH2 slice 3 THERE WAS NO SUCH THING, WHICH
// SILENTLY DROPPED WHOLE TEST CASES.** The exit code used to be the one half of a run expectation with no
// "unpinned" state: `scanTestFromMarker` pushed a test only `if haveExitCode`, so a `/specs` case that
// pins ONLY stdout — which is legal, and which the C# reference runs (`SpecParser.cs:171`, `ExitCode` is
// nullable and the success expectation is built from ANY result block) — was consumed as a full test
// boundary and then thrown away. No test, no golden, and NOTHING IN THE OUTPUT SAYING SO: the case simply
// was not in the count.
//
// ⚠ **MEASURED WHEN IT FIRST BIT: all 3 cases of `specs/init-from-literal.md` vanished** — the file
// parsed, the suite reported `no tests selected matching --filter=init-from-literal/`, and a reader
// checking the summary would have seen a green suite that had never compiled the feature. A sweep of the
// whole committed corpus at the same time found NO other file affected (every other case carries an
// ```exitcode or ```maxoncstderr block), so this drop was latent rather than active — but it is exactly
// the shape `RequiredDataFence`'s header calls out: a gate that cannot see its own property must not
// report PASS.
//
// ⇒ the exit code is now the same UNION the other three halves are, and the push condition is "a source
// block and AT LEAST ONE result check", which is the reference's rule.
export union SpecExitCode
	unpinned
	pinned(value ExitCodeValue)
end 'SpecExitCode'

// The expected outcome of one test. A UNION (not a struct with presence flags)
// so a test asserts EXACTLY one of two mutually exclusive things — the compiled
// program RAN, OR the compiler rejected the source with `text` (a ```maxoncstderr
// block) — with no sentinel "unset" state.
//
// The `run` arm carries EVERY half of a run expectation (exit code, stderr, stdout, the section
// pins), because they ACCOMPANY one another — a case may pin any subset, and most pin an exit code
// plus one stream. `specs/safety.md` asserts that
// `100 / zero` exits 1 AND prints a panic to stderr; `specs/static-variables.md` asserts that
// `data-section-i64-8byte` exits 0 AND lays down `i64 42`; `specs/string-type.md` asserts that
// `print-literal` exits 0 AND writes `Hello, World!` to stdout. Each is one indivisible claim about
// one build-and-run. Modelling any of them as a parallel arm would let a test assert an exit code or a
// stream but never both, which is precisely what those cases do assert. The C# reference says the same
// thing with independent nullable fields (`SpecParser.cs:171` — a success expectation has optional
// ExitCode/Stdout/Stderr/RequiredData, mutually exclusive with a compile-error expectation); this is
// that shape with the "unset" states the reference leaves as `null` made unrepresentable.
export union SpecExpectation
	run(exitCode SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout, sectionPins SpecSectionPinArray)
	compilerError(text String)
end 'SpecExpectation'

// One named SECTION of a ```maxon block — a `// --- file: <name>` file of a multi-file test, or a
// `// --- stdlib-overlay: <name>` overlay. ONE type for both, because they carry the same three facts
// and the runner applies `mergedLineOffset` to them identically; what differs is only WHERE the text
// is written, which is the array a section lands in.
export type SpecSourceFile
	// The path from the marker, RELATIVE (`app/main.maxon`) — to the test's own scratch directory for
	// a file, to the staged `stdlib/` for an overlay. Written there, subdirectories and all.
	export let name as String
	// The file's own text, trimmed — exactly what is written to disk and compiled.
	export let source as String
	// Add this to a line number the COMPILER reports for this file, and you get the line number in
	// the MERGED fragment — which is what the golden shows and what every `/specs` expectation was
	// authored against (`specs/fragments/<spec>/<test>.test:11:9`). Without it a multi-file
	// diagnostic names a line nobody can find: the compiler saw `main.maxon:2`, the reviewer is
	// holding a merged file where that line is 11.
	//
	// It counts the marker line, everything above it, and the one `// Test: <name>` header line the
	// fragment carries — the same arithmetic the C# reference does in `TestRunner.ComputeSplitFileMap`.
	export let mergedLineOffset as LineIndex

	export static function create(name String, source String, mergedLineOffset LineIndex) returns SpecSourceFile
		return Self{name: name, source: source, mergedLineOffset: mergedLineOffset}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecSourceFile'

export typealias SpecSourceFileArray = Array with SpecSourceFile

// The `targetToString` forms a `<!-- targets: … -->` marker restricts a test to. EMPTY means "no
// restriction" — the common case, and the literal count of targets a marker-less test names — so it
// is not a sentinel. Named for its PURPOSE (the target keys), not its type.
export typealias TargetKeyArray = Array with String

// The arguments an `<!-- Args: … -->` marker hands the compiled program, in order. EMPTY means the
// program is spawned with argv[0] alone — the ordinary case, and the literal count of arguments a
// marker-less test names — so it is not a sentinel. Named for its PURPOSE, as `TargetKeyArray` is.
export typealias ProgramArgArray = Array with String

// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER A CASE ASSERTS A DEBUG-STREAM TRACE, WHICH FAMILY, AND WHAT IT EXPECTS — THREE STATES IN
// ONE FIELD.** A case can be in capture MODE with no golden yet (the authoring state: a bare
// `<!-- MmTrace -->` / `<!-- LogTrace -->` marker, which `--update-required` turns into a block), or in
// mode WITH one, or not in mode at all. Two fields — a bool and a String — would need "" to mean "no
// block", and an EMPTY trace is a legitimate expectation: it is exactly what a program that allocates
// nothing must produce. A sentinel there would make that case unwritable, which is the shape
// `SpecStdout.unpinned` exists to avoid one stream over.
//
// ⚠ **THE FAMILY RIDES BOTH LIVE ARMS AND NOT THE TEST**, because it is only meaningful when there is a
// capture at all: a field beside this one would need a value for `off`, which is the same sentinel this
// union exists to avoid one level up.
export union SpecTraceCapture
	// Not a capture case: built and run exactly as any other.
	off
	// In capture mode with no golden. The program is built with `--debugstream` and run under the monitor,
	// and the trace is compared against NOTHING — so the case fails unless the family it names is silent,
	// and `--update-required` mints the block that makes it a real assertion.
	unpinned(family TraceFamily)
	// In capture mode with a golden, normalized and compared line for line.
	pinned(family TraceFamily, trace String)
end 'SpecTraceCapture'

// The family a capture case names. `off` has none — a caller that reaches here has already established
// the case IS a capture (`SpecTestRunner.traceCaptureMode`), so an `off` here is a caller that did not.
// A marker RAISES the capture mode and never lowers it: a `pinned` set by a block earlier in the case must
// not become `unpinned` again, so a marker's family is the case's only when no block has already named
// one. Written once because both markers ask it, and because the arm that must NOT fire is the easy one to
// get wrong.
function raisedTraceCapture(current SpecTraceCapture, family TraceFamily) returns SpecTraceCapture
	return match current 'raise'
		off gives SpecTraceCapture.unpinned(family)
		unpinned(existing) gives SpecTraceCapture.unpinned(existing)
		pinned(existing, trace) gives SpecTraceCapture.pinned(existing, trace: trace)
	end 'raise'
end 'raisedTraceCapture'

export function traceCaptureFamilyOf(capture SpecTraceCapture) returns TraceFamily
	return match capture 'mode'
		off panic("traceCaptureFamilyOf: a case not in capture mode names no family — ask `traceCaptureMode` before asking this")
		unpinned(family) gives family
		pinned(family, _) gives family
	end 'mode'
end 'traceCaptureFamilyOf'

// ⭐ **WHAT A CASE NEEDS FROM THE NETWORK — see `NetworkMarker` for why this is a per-case fact.**
//
// A UNION rather than a bool, because the marker carries a VALUE and the value is the thing selection
// acts on: `network: live` says "a real external host", and a second answer (a loopback server this
// harness starts, say) would be a third state rather than the negation of one. A bool would throw the
// distinction away at parse time, and every reader of it would then be asking a question the field can no
// longer answer. It also makes every consumer's `match` total, which is how a new arm becomes a compile
// error instead of a silently-selected case.
export union SpecNetwork
	// Reaches no external host. Selected by every run, which is every case not carrying the marker.
	none
	// Opens a socket to a REAL EXTERNAL HOST. Left out of a default run and selected under `--network`.
	live
end 'SpecNetwork'

// One active test: its name, its ```maxon source VERBATIM, and its expectation.
export type SpecTest
	export let name as String
	// The block VERBATIM — markers and all. This is what the golden `.test` fragment shows and, for
	// a single-file test, what is compiled. It is never re-derived from `sourceFiles`: the merged
	// text is the thing the spec author wrote, and a round-trip through the split would let the two
	// drift.
	export let source as String
	export let expectation as SpecExpectation
	// The `// --- file:` sections, or EMPTY for the ordinary single-file test. Empty is not a
	// sentinel — it is the literal count of file markers in the block, and "no markers" is exactly
	// what a single-file test is.
	export let sourceFiles as SpecSourceFileArray
	// The `// --- stdlib-overlay:` sections, or EMPTY for every test that compiles against the
	// checkout's own `stdlib/` — which is all but a handful. Empty is the literal count of overlay
	// markers, not a sentinel, exactly as `sourceFiles` is.
	export let stdlibOverlays as SpecSourceFileArray
	// The `targetToString` forms this test is restricted to (a `<!-- targets: … -->` marker), or EMPTY
	// for the ordinary test that runs on every target. The runner SKIPS a test whose non-empty list
	// omits the effective target (`SpecTestRunner.markerAllowsTarget`) — the same treatment a draft or
	// filtered-out test gets, never a failure. ⚠ Distinct from a SKIPPED result, which is a test that
	// WAS selected, WAS compiled and was refused by the target; that one is counted and reported.
	export let targets as TargetKeyArray
	// The emitted runtime functions this test's golden renders (a ```RequiredRuntime block). It rides
	// on the TEST rather than on the expectation because it is not something the run is checked
	// against: it changes what the COMPILE is asked to print, which is a property of how this test is
	// built (like `targets`), not of what its execution must do.
	export let runtimeNames as RuntimeFunctionNameArray
	// The arguments this case's program is SPAWNED with (an `<!-- Args: … -->` marker), or EMPTY for
	// the ordinary test that reads no argv. Like `targets` and `runtimeNames` it rides on the TEST
	// rather than on the expectation: it changes how the run is PERFORMED, not what the run must
	// produce.
	export let programArgs as ProgramArgArray

	// Whether this case asserts the green-thread async TRACE (an `<!-- AsyncTrace -->` marker). It sits HERE
	// rather than on `SpecExpectation` because it is not part of what the run must PRODUCE — it changes how the
	// program is BUILT (`--async-trace`) and how its stderr is COMPARED, which is the same reason `targets`,
	// `runtimeNames` and `programArgs` live here. v1 carries it on both the expectation and the test, which is
	// one fact in two places.
	export let asyncTrace as bool

	// Whether this case asserts a DEBUG-STREAM trace, which family, and what it expects (see
	// `SpecTraceCapture`). It sits beside `asyncTrace` for that field's stated reason and it is the same
	// kind of fact: it changes how the program is BUILT (`--debugstream`) and how its output is COMPARED
	// (under `maxon monitor`, through `normalizeTrace`), rather than what the run must produce.
	//
	// ⚠ The EXPECTATION rides here too, unlike `asyncTrace`'s, because a capture block IS the
	// assertion — there is no other slot on `SpecExpectation` it could land in, and putting the mode on
	// the test and the golden on the expectation would be the one fact in two places this file's
	// `asyncTrace` note already refuses.
	export let traceCapture as SpecTraceCapture

	// What this case needs from the network (a `<!-- network: … -->` marker). It rides HERE for the reason
	// `targets` does and is the same kind of fact: it decides whether this run SELECTS the case at all,
	// which is a property of the case rather than of what its execution must produce. Unlike `targets` it
	// is answered against the RUN MODE (`--network`) instead of against the effective target — see
	// `SpecTestRunner.networkModeAllows`.
	export let network as SpecNetwork

	// The split is derived HERE, from the very source that will be compiled, so a test can never
	// exist with a file list that disagrees with its text.
	export static function create(name String, source String, expectation SpecExpectation, targets TargetKeyArray, runtimeNames RuntimeFunctionNameArray, programArgs ProgramArgArray, asyncTrace bool, traceCapture SpecTraceCapture, network SpecNetwork) returns SpecTest
		let sections = splitSourceSections(name, source: source)
		return Self{name: name, source: source, expectation: expectation, sourceFiles: sections.files, stdlibOverlays: sections.stdlibOverlays, targets: targets, runtimeNames: runtimeNames, programArgs: programArgs, asyncTrace: asyncTrace, traceCapture: traceCapture, network: network}
	end 'create'

	// True when this test's block declares `// --- file:` sections — i.e. it compiles as a
	// DIRECTORY of files rather than as one fragment file.
	export function isMultiFile() returns bool
		return self.sourceFiles.count() > 0
	end 'isMultiFile'

	// ⭐ True when this case pins the bytes of a LINKED SECTION (a ```RequiredData / ```RequiredRdata
	// block), which is what a target restriction for such a case is DERIVED from — see
	// `SpecTestRunner.targetCanCheckSectionPins` and `LinkedSection.targetHasLinkedSections`. The
	// declaration lives on the CASE that carries the block rather than in a `<!-- targets: … -->`
	// marker beside it, because a marker is a second copy of "which formats have a section", and the
	// copy that goes missing takes a whole lane down with it.
	//
	// A `compilerError` case answers a structural FALSE, not a default: `scanTestFromMarker`'s
	// `pinsOnARejectedCase` refuses a pin on a case that never links, so such a case cannot carry one.
	export function pinsLinkedSection() returns bool
		return match self.expectation 'e'
			run(_, _, _, sectionPins) gives not sectionPins.isEmpty()
			compilerError gives false
		end 'e'
	end 'pinsLinkedSection'
end 'SpecTest'

export typealias SpecTestArray = Array with SpecTest

// A parsed spec file: its frontmatter `status` (the runner skips `draft`), every
// ACTIVE test, and how many cases were shelved behind `<!-- disabled-test: … -->`.
// (The path is deliberately NOT stored — the caller already holds the FilePath it
// passed to `parseSpecFile`, so a copy here would be dead.)
export type SpecFile
	export let status as String
	export let tests as SpecTestArray
	export let disabledCount as DisabledTestCount

	export static function create(status String, tests SpecTestArray, disabledCount DisabledTestCount) returns SpecFile
		return Self{status: status, tests: tests, disabledCount: disabledCount}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecFile'

// What one pass over a spec's `## Tests` region found: the runnable tests, and the
// count of cases deliberately shelved. Two values, one traversal — returning both
// keeps `extractTests` a single scan rather than a scan plus a separate re-count,
// which could disagree with it.
type ExtractedTests
	export let tests as SpecTestArray
	export let disabledCount as DisabledTestCount

	export static function create(tests SpecTestArray, disabledCount DisabledTestCount) returns ExtractedTests
		return Self{tests: tests, disabledCount: disabledCount}
	end 'create'
end 'ExtractedTests'

// One fenced code block: its content (fence lines excluded), and the line index
// immediately AFTER its closing ``` — the single unit the block reader returns
// so every block type (source / exitcode / maxoncstderr) shares one routine
// instead of copy-pasted fence walking.
type FenceBlock
	export let text as String
	export let nextIndex as LineIndex

	export static function create(text String, nextIndex LineIndex) returns FenceBlock
		return Self{text: text, nextIndex: nextIndex}
	end 'create'
end 'FenceBlock'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO BLOCKS THAT MAY COMPETE FOR ONE EXPECTATION SLOT** — a portable fence (```stdout) and a
// target-qualified one (```Stdout:x64-windows) — held APART until the whole run of a test's blocks has
// been read. The two may appear in EITHER order (see `TargetStdoutFencePrefix`), so "the qualified block
// WINS" cannot be expressed by letting a later assignment overwrite an earlier one.
//
// ⚠ **ONE type for all THREE slots that have a qualified spelling** — the compile-error text, the run's
// stderr, the run's stdout. Each is a String under a "did the spec write one" flag and each resolves by
// the identical preference, so the preference is stated ONCE. Growing the mechanism from one slot to three
// by copying the pair of variables and the resolve function beside it would have been this project's
// signature bug — one fact written down three times — in the file that decides what every spec MEANS.
//
// ⚠ The two flags are NOT `portable.isEmpty()`: an EMPTY block is a legitimate claim (a ```stdout block
// with nothing in it asserts the program printed nothing — see `SpecStdout`), so inferring presence from
// the text would be exactly the sentinel those unions exist to avoid.
type TargetPreferredText
	var portable as String
	var qualified as String
	var havePortable as bool
	var haveQualified as bool

	static function create() returns TargetPreferredText
		return Self{portable: "", qualified: "", havePortable: false, haveQualified: false}
	end 'create'

	// A LATER block of the same kind replaces an earlier one, which is what a plain assignment always did
	// here — two portable ```stdout blocks in one case are a spec-authoring mistake, not two claims, and
	// unlike a section pin they cannot accumulate (a run has one stdout).
	export function recordPortable(text String)
		portable = text
		havePortable = true
	end 'recordPortable'

	export function recordQualified(text String)
		qualified = text
		haveQualified = true
	end 'recordQualified'

	// Did the spec write EITHER block for this slot? The push condition, and what keeps `resolved` from
	// being asked a question it has no answer to.
	export function isPinned() returns bool
		return haveQualified or havePortable
	end 'isPinned'

	// The block naming THIS run's target when the spec wrote one, else the portable block — the
	// reference's `??` (`SpecParser.cs:190`), spelled once for all three slots.
	export function resolved() returns String
		if not self.isPinned() 'nothingWritten'
			panic("SpecParser.TargetPreferredText.resolved: this slot holds no block. Callers gate on `isPinned` first, and an unwritten slot cannot fall back to the empty String — an EMPTY block is itself a claim (the stream was silent), so that value is already taken")
		end 'nothingWritten'

		return qualified if haveQualified else portable
	end 'resolved'
end 'TargetPreferredText'

// Read the spec file at `path` and extract its frontmatter status + active
// tests. Throws `readError` when the file cannot be read (the runner surfaces
// it), reusing the driver's IO error union rather than inventing a parallel one.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE RUN'S TARGET, because THREE fences' meaning depends on it** — `` ```Stdout:<target> ``
// and its `` ```Stderr: `` / `` ```Maxoncstderr: `` twins resolve HERE, the way the reference resolves the
// one it has (`SpecParser.cs:177`), so a `SpecTest` carries an already-decided expectation and no reader
// downstream can resolve it a second, different way. It is
// deliberately NOT the treatment `<!-- targets: … -->` gets — that marker decides whether the test is in
// this run's SET at all, which is a selection the RUNNER owns and reports ("skipped"); this one decides
// what the test's expectation IS, which is the parse's own answer.
export function parseSpecFile(path FilePath, effectiveTargetKey String) returns SpecFile throws CompileError
	let content = try File.readText(path) otherwise throw CompileError.readError(path)
	let lines = content.split("\n")
	let extracted = extractTests(lines, specPath: path, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey)
	return SpecFile.create(parseStatus(lines), tests: extracted.tests, disabledCount: extracted.disabledCount)
end 'parseSpecFile'

// Read the `status:` key out of the YAML frontmatter (the leading `--- … ---` block). "" when
// there is no frontmatter or no status key — the runner skips only the literal "draft", so ""
// runs normally.
//
// ⚠⚠ **`status: selfhosted` DOES NOT SUSPEND A SPEC HERE, AND READING IT AS "SUSPENDED" HAS ALREADY
// COST A RUNG.** It is INHERITED metadata: in `/specs` it means "the C# bootstrap hands this file to
// the other runner" and the bootstrap's `SpecParser.SuspendingStatuses` skips it, counting it in a
// `Suspended: N spec file(s) holding M test(s)` trailer. A spec ported into `specs-shv2/` is copied
// BYTE-IDENTICAL, frontmatter and all, so that line rides along — describing a decision the BOOTSTRAP
// made about the `/specs` copy, not one about this corpus. THIRTY-TWO files carry it here today
// (`arithmetic`, `assignment`, `basics`, `break`, `division`, `functions`, `if-statements`,
// `variables`, `while-loops`, …) and every one of them RUNS and PASSES; honouring it
// would silently delete the language's core cases from the suite. ⇒ the only status that means
// anything to THIS runner is `draft`, and a suspension in `specs-shv2/` is spelled by disabling the
// cases (`<!-- disabled-test: … -->`), which is counted.
function parseStatus(lines StringArray) returns String
	var status = ""

	let lineCount = lines.count()
	if lineCount == 0 'noLines'
		return status
	end 'noLines'

	let firstLine = try lines.get(0) otherwise ""
	if firstLine.trim() != FrontmatterFence 'noFrontmatter'
		return status
	end 'noFrontmatter'

	var i = 1
	while i < lineCount 'scan'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.parseStatus: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the while guard i<lineCount")

		let trimmed = line.trim()
		if trimmed == FrontmatterFence 'endFrontmatter'
			break
		end 'endFrontmatter'

		if trimmed.startsWith(StatusKey) 'isStatus'
			status = valueAfterKey(trimmed, key: StatusKey)
		end 'isStatus'

		i = i + 1
	end 'scan'

	return status
end 'parseStatus'

// The trimmed remainder of `line` after a literal `key` prefix. `line` is known
// to start with `key` (the caller gates on `startsWith`), so advancing
// `key.byteLength()` ASCII positions lands exactly past the key.
function valueAfterKey(line String, key String) returns String
	var pos = line.startIndex()
	for _ in 0 upto key.byteLength() 'skipKey'
		pos = try line.indexAfter(pos) otherwise return ""
	end 'skipKey'
	return line.slice(pos, endIndex: line.endIndex()).trim()
end 'valueAfterKey'

// Whether `line` is a heading that ENDS the active-test region — the single decision
// site for `RegionEndHeadings`, which states the stop set. An ordinary `## ` heading is
// not one: a spec is free to organize its cases under section headings, and the scan
// walks straight over them.
function endsActiveTestRegion(line String) returns bool
	for heading in RegionEndHeadings 'stops'
		if line.startsWith(heading) 'isStop'
			return true
		end 'isStop'
	end 'stops'

	return false
end 'endsActiveTestRegion'

// Extract every ACTIVE test, and count the disabled ones. The scanned region is the
// `## Tests` section only: from the line after the `## Tests` heading to the next STOP
// HEADING (or EOF). `## Deferred` is that stop, so no deferred test is ever reached — see
// the deferred-skip convention at the top of this file, and `RegionEndHeadings` for why
// an ordinary section heading must NOT end the region.
function extractTests(lines StringArray, specPath FilePath, effectiveTargetKey String) returns ExtractedTests
	var tests = SpecTestArray.create()
	var disabledCount = 0 as DisabledTestCount
	let lineCount = lines.count()

	// Locate the `## Tests` heading; the active-test region begins on the next line.
	var i = 0
	var inTests = false
	while i < lineCount 'find'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.extractTests: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the while guard i<lineCount")

		if line.startsWith(TestsHeading) 'foundTests'
			inTests = true
			i = i + 1
			break
		end 'foundTests'

		i = i + 1
	end 'find'

	if not inTests 'noTestsSection'
		return ExtractedTests.create(tests, disabledCount: disabledCount)
	end 'noTestsSection'

	while i < lineCount 'scan'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.extractTests: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the while guard i<lineCount")

		// A STOP heading — `## Deferred` — ends the active-test region. Any other `## `
		// heading is an ordinary section inside `## Tests` and is walked over below.
		if endsActiveTestRegion(line) 'sectionEnd'
			break
		end 'sectionEnd'

		// A DISABLED case is scanned by the very same routine as an active one — it
		// is a full test boundary, just one whose result is thrown away. Sharing the
		// scan is the point: a second "skip a disabled test" walker would be free to
		// disagree with this one about where a test's blocks end, and the failure
		// mode of that disagreement is a shelved case silently RUNNING.
		let openings = markerOpeningsIn(line)
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: DisabledTestMarker) 'isDisabledMarker'
			disabledCount = disabledCount + 1
			i = scanTestFromMarker(lines, markerIndex: i, markerLine: line, tests: tests, active: false, specPath: specPath, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey)
		end 'isDisabledMarker' else if openingsCarry(openings, marker: TestMarker) 'isMarker'
			i = scanTestFromMarker(lines, markerIndex: i, markerLine: line, tests: tests, active: true, specPath: specPath, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey)
		end 'isMarker' else 'notMarker'
			i = i + 1
		end 'notMarker'
	end 'scan'

	return ExtractedTests.create(tests, disabledCount: disabledCount)
end 'extractTests'

// ⭐⭐ **A TEST MARKER THAT IS NEVER CLOSED IS REFUSED — because the way a spec author COMMENTS A CASE
// OUT is to leave the `-->` until after its fences, and this parser used to RUN such a case.**
//
// `markerValue` answers with everything to the end of the line when it finds no `MarkerEnd`, and
// that fallback is right for it — it reads six different markers and cannot decide for all of them. For
// a TEST marker it is a wrong answer with teeth: the name parses, the case becomes ACTIVE, and the
// harness compiles and runs a program the file says is switched off. Every other marker's fallback is
// harmless by comparison (`markerValue` trims, so an unterminated `<!-- targets: x64-windows` yields the
// same key a closed one would); this is the only one that CREATES A CASE.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED 2026-08-23, AND IT IS A DIVERGENCE FROM THE REFERENCE RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.** The
// bootstrap's `SpecParser.cs` `TestMarkerRegex` is `<!--\s*test:\s*(\S+)\s*-->` — it REQUIRES the end
// token, and `\s*` cannot span the prose line such a case carries — so it correctly declines the
// marker. `specs/unary-operators.md` held one, and the bootstrap ran three cases of that file with
// `negate-float` NOT among them, while shv2 ran the identical marker in its own copy as a live case.
// One `.md`, two meanings, and the silent half was ours.
//
// Asked at the ONE door both kinds of marker go through, so a shelved case is refused identically —
// `extractTests` routes `<!-- disabled-test: … -->` here too, and the two must not come to disagree
// about what a well-formed marker line is.
//
// It PANICS in the parent, before a worker exists, exactly as this file's other spec-authoring
// refusals do; `HarnessSelfTest`'s `unterminated-test-marker` fixture is what proves it still fires.
function refuseUnterminatedTestMarker(markerLine String, marker String, markerIndex LineIndex, specPath FilePath)
	// ⛔ **ASKED OF THIS MARKER'S OWN OPENING, NOT OF THE LINE.** It used to be `markerLine.contains`, and
	// on `<!-- note --> <!-- test: foo` that is satisfied by the OTHER comment's closer while the test
	// marker runs off the end of the line — the exact shape this refusal exists to catch, walking through
	// the check meant to stop it. Zero such lines in either corpus, which is how the last two got in.
	match markerOpeningAmong(markerOpeningsIn(markerLine), marker: marker) 'opening'
		absent then panic("SpecParser.refuseUnterminatedTestMarker: '{markerLine}' was recognized as carrying '{marker}' moments ago and no longer does — recognition and this refusal have come apart, which is the one thing they may never do")
		at(text) then refuseUnclosedOpening(text, markerIndex: markerIndex, specPath: specPath)
	end 'opening'
end 'refuseUnterminatedTestMarker'

// The refusal itself, once the marker's own opening is in hand.
function refuseUnclosedOpening(opening String, markerIndex LineIndex, specPath FilePath)
	if opening.contains(MarkerEnd) 'properlyClosed'
		return
	end 'properlyClosed'

	panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}:{markerIndex + 1}: this test marker is never closed — its own '{CommentOpen}' has no '{MarkerEnd}' after it, so everything past the '{MarkerNameSep}' would be read as the case NAME: '{opening}'. That is how a case its author COMMENTED OUT gets run: the `-->` ends up below the case's fences, the marker parses anyway, and the harness compiles and runs a program the file says is switched off. The reference parser declines this marker outright, so the same file already means two different things to two compilers. Close the marker on its own line — and if the case was meant to be shelved, delete the trailing `-->` as well and write it as a `{DisabledTestMarker} NAME -->` naming the rung that unlocks it")
end 'refuseUnclosedOpening'

// Which of the two spellings a case's marker line was recognized by. `extractTests` asks the disabled
// one first and the active one second, and this says the same thing from the `active` flag it already
// passes — so the refusal below interrogates the opening that was actually matched, rather than
// guessing which of the two it was.
function testMarkerSpellingFor(active bool) returns String
	if active 'liveCase'
		return TestMarker
	end 'liveCase'

	return DisabledTestMarker
end 'testMarkerSpellingFor'

// Scan one test starting at its marker line, returning the line index to resume the
// outer scan from. When `active`, the parsed test is pushed onto `tests`; when not
// (a `<!-- disabled-test: … -->` case) it is scanned identically and DISCARDED, so
// the shelved case is skipped by consuming exactly the blocks a live test would
// have consumed rather than by a separate, driftable walker.
//
// A test is pushed when a ```maxon source block AND AT LEAST ONE result block were
// seen before the next marker / section boundary (the "marker + source + expected,
// contiguous" active-test contract). ANY result block qualifies — an exit code, either
// stream, or a `.data` pin — which is the C# reference's rule (`SpecParser.cs:171`) and
// NOT what this used to require; see `SpecExitCode` for the three cases it silently
// dropped. A source block with NO result block is not a runnable test and PANICS naming
// it, because it is a spec-authoring error whose only other outcome is a case that looks
// present and tests nothing.
//
// The scan reads the test's WHOLE block run and builds the expectation once at the end,
// rather than returning at the first expected block it recognizes. It has to: a run
// expectation is spread across TWO fences (```exitcode then ```stderr — see
// `SpecExpectation`), so a scan that returned at the exitcode would leave the stderr
// block to the outer loop, which — finding no marker in it — would walk over it line by
// line and silently drop the assertion. A spec that pins a panic would then pass on the
// exit code alone, which is the one failure mode a panic spec exists to catch.
function scanTestFromMarker(lines StringArray, markerIndex LineIndex, markerLine String, tests SpecTestArray, active bool, specPath FilePath, effectiveTargetKey String) returns LineIndex
	refuseUnterminatedTestMarker(markerLine, marker: testMarkerSpellingFor(active), markerIndex: markerIndex, specPath: specPath)

	let lineCount = lines.count()
	let name = markerValue(markerLine)
	var source = ""
	var haveSource = false
	var exitCode = SpecExitCode.unpinned
	// The three slots a spec may state TWICE — once portably and once for a named target. Each holds both
	// blocks until the whole run of a test's blocks has been read, because the two fences may appear in
	// either order; `TargetPreferredText` carries the preference and the reason.
	var compilerError = TargetPreferredText.create()
	var stderr = TargetPreferredText.create()
	var stdout = TargetPreferredText.create()
	var sectionPins = SpecSectionPinArray.create()
	var targets = TargetKeyArray.create()
	var runtimeNames = RuntimeFunctionNameArray.create()
	var programArgs = ProgramArgArray.create()
	var asyncTrace = false
	// Starts `off`; the MARKER raises it to `unpinned` and the BLOCK to `pinned`. A case carrying both
	// ends `pinned` whichever order they appear in, because the block never lowers the mode.
	var traceCapture = SpecTraceCapture.off
	// `none` unless a `<!-- network: … -->` marker says otherwise — the ordinary case, and the literal
	// answer for a case that reaches no host, not a default standing in for an unread marker.
	var network = SpecNetwork.none
	var i = markerIndex + 1

	while i < lineCount 'blocks'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.scanTestFromMarker: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the while guard i<lineCount")

		// The next marker of EITHER kind, or a section boundary, ends this test. Leave
		// `i` ON that line so the outer scan re-examines it. Disabled markers must end
		// a test just as active ones do, or an active case followed by a shelved one
		// would swallow the shelved case's blocks and mis-read its expectation.
		//
		// ⚠ ANY `## ` heading ends a TEST — deliberately wider than `endsActiveTestRegion`,
		// which ends the whole REGION. The last case before `## Stage 3` stops at that
		// heading (its blocks cannot span it), and the region then continues underneath.
		if line.startsWith(SectionHeadingPrefix) 'sectionEnd'
			break
		end 'sectionEnd'
		// Computed ONCE and asked of every family below — see `markerOpeningsIn` for why that matters here
		// in particular: this loop runs over every line of every case and asks eight questions of each.
		let openings = markerOpeningsIn(line)

		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: TestMarker) or openingsCarry(openings, marker: DisabledTestMarker) 'nextMarker'
			break
		end 'nextMarker'

		// The per-target gate — one of the comment lines that may sit between this test's marker and
		// its first fence. It is neither a fence nor a next-test marker, so without this it would fall
		// through to `i = i + 1` and be silently ignored (which is exactly how safety.md's four markers
		// went unread until this rung). Parsed here so a disabled case consumes it identically to a live
		// one; the disabled case then discards the whole test, targets and all.
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: TargetsMarker) 'targetsMarker'
			targets = parseTargetKeys(line)
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'targetsMarker'

		// The runtime arguments — the other comment line that may sit between this test's marker and its
		// first fence, read here for `TargetsMarker`'s reasons exactly: it is neither a fence nor a next-test
		// marker, so without an arm it falls through to `i = i + 1` and is silently ignored, and a disabled
		// case must consume it identically to a live one.
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: ArgsMarker) 'argsMarker'
			programArgs = parseProgramArgs(line)
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'argsMarker'

		// The valueless flag marker. Consumed identically to the four above — including on a DISABLED case, so
		// shelving a trace case does not leave its marker to fall through as unread prose.
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: AsyncTraceMarker) 'asyncTraceMarker'
			asyncTrace = true
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'asyncTraceMarker'

		// The other two valueless flag markers, consumed identically — including on a DISABLED case, so
		// shelving a capture case does not leave its marker to fall through as unread prose. Each only RAISES
		// the mode; see `raisedTraceCapture` for what that means when a block has already named a family.
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: MmTraceMarker) 'mmTraceMarker'
			traceCapture = raisedTraceCapture(traceCapture, family: TraceFamily.mm)
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'mmTraceMarker'

		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: LogTraceMarker) 'logTraceMarker'
			traceCapture = raisedTraceCapture(traceCapture, family: TraceFamily.log)
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'logTraceMarker'

		// The network gate the author SPELLED, consumed identically to the five above — including on a
		// DISABLED case, so shelving a live-network case does not leave its marker to fall through as
		// unread prose. The VALUE is refused rather than defaulted; see `parseNetworkValue`.
		if openingsCarry(openings, marker: NetworkMarker) 'networkMarker'
			network = parseNetworkValue(line, specPath: specPath, testName: name)
			i = i + 1
			continue
		end 'networkMarker'

		// ⛔⛔ **A PROSE SENTENCE THAT HAPPENS TO BEGIN WITH A FENCE PREFIX IS NOT A FENCE, AND EVERY ARM
		// BELOW MATCHES BY PREFIX.** `` ```RequiredRuntime block, one name per line: `` — a real line in
		// `emitted-runtime-body.md`, where an author meant inline code and typed three backticks —
		// `startsWith(RequiredRuntimeFence)`, so it would be CONSUMED as a `RequiredRuntime` block and its
		// prose read as function names. Today that line is harmless for one reason only: it sits above
		// `## Tests`, where this scan never reaches. That is a coincidence of where it happens to sit, not
		// a property of anything, and the terminating arm below has just raised the price of tripping it
		// from a silent skip to a whole-suite abort.
		//
		// The rule is derived rather than listed, so it needs no second copy of the fence vocabulary: EVERY
		// tag in this grammar is a single token, and the shape being caught is a SENTENCE. Asked before the
		// arms because the arms are what would claim it, and for a DISABLED case as well as a live one —
		// this is a line that is not a fence at all, which is true however the case is marked, and
		// `pushRuntimeNames` panics just as hard while scanning a shelved case.
		// ⭐ ONE trim, read by BOTH fence questions — the one above and the terminating arm at the bottom —
		// so they cannot come to disagree about what "opens a fence" is. They already did: this arm
		// trimmed and `isUnimplementedFenceOpen` did not, so INDENTING an unreadable fence by two spaces
		// walked straight past the terminating arm and the block was skipped in silence again (measured
		// 2026-08-06, BATCH29 review: `  ```mm-trace` in a live case, suite green, exit 0). Markdown
		// allows a fence up to three columns in and a fence nested in a list item further still, so that
		// is a spelling an author reaches by accident, not a contrivance.
		let fenceLine = line.trim()

		if fenceOpenCarriesProse(fenceLine) 'proseLineOpeningWithAFencePrefix'
			panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}:{i + 1}: this line begins with a fence delimiter and its info string contains a space, so it is a PROSE line that every fence arm would claim by prefix and read as a block body: '{fenceLine}'. Every fence tag in this grammar is a single token. Write the name as inline code (one backtick) if it is prose, or make the line a real fence")
		end 'proseLineOpeningWithAFencePrefix'

		// CompilerError MUST be checked before Source: "```maxoncstderr" also
		// startsWith "```maxon", so the source arm would otherwise consume it. It is the ONLY ordering
		// constraint among the fences — every other pair is in no prefix relation at all, which the
		// `--- Spec-markdown grammar tokens ---` header argues for the whole set.
		if line.startsWith(CompilerErrorFence) 'compilerErr'
			i = recordPortableText(lines, openIndex: i, slot: compilerError)
			continue
		end 'compilerErr'

		if line.startsWith(RuntimeStderrFence) 'runtimeStderr'
			i = recordPortableText(lines, openIndex: i, slot: stderr)
			continue
		end 'runtimeStderr'

		if line.startsWith(StdoutFence) 'runtimeStdout'
			i = recordPortableText(lines, openIndex: i, slot: stdout)
			continue
		end 'runtimeStdout'

		// The three TARGET-QUALIFIED blocks, each the twin of the portable arm above it. One routine, one
		// target comparison — see `recordTargetQualifiedText` for why every such block is READ whatever
		// target it names.
		if line.startsWith(TargetCompilerErrorFencePrefix) 'targetCompilerErr'
			i = recordTargetQualifiedText(lines, openIndex: i, openLine: line, fencePrefix: TargetCompilerErrorFencePrefix, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey, slot: compilerError)
			continue
		end 'targetCompilerErr'

		if line.startsWith(TargetStderrFencePrefix) 'targetStderr'
			i = recordTargetQualifiedText(lines, openIndex: i, openLine: line, fencePrefix: TargetStderrFencePrefix, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey, slot: stderr)
			continue
		end 'targetStderr'

		if line.startsWith(TargetStdoutFencePrefix) 'targetStdout'
			i = recordTargetQualifiedText(lines, openIndex: i, openLine: line, fencePrefix: TargetStdoutFencePrefix, effectiveTargetKey: effectiveTargetKey, slot: stdout)
			continue
		end 'targetStdout'

		// The two section pins differ in exactly one thing — WHICH section they name — so the block
		// reading and the recording are written once and the difference is the argument. See
		// `RequiredDataFence` for why the two fences cannot swallow one another.
		if line.startsWith(RequiredDataFence) 'globalsPin'
			i = pushSectionPin(lines, openIndex: i, testName: name, fence: RequiredDataFence, section: LinkedSectionKind.globals, pins: sectionPins)
			continue
		end 'globalsPin'

		if line.startsWith(RequiredRdataFence) 'readOnlyPin'
			i = pushSectionPin(lines, openIndex: i, testName: name, fence: RequiredRdataFence, section: LinkedSectionKind.readOnly, pins: sectionPins)
			continue
		end 'readOnlyPin'

		if line.startsWith(RequiredRuntimeFence) 'runtimeBlock'
			i = pushRuntimeNames(lines, openIndex: i, testName: name, names: runtimeNames)
			continue
		end 'runtimeBlock'

		// The two capture goldens. Either is a result check in its own right (see `MmTraceFence`), and either
		// puts the case into capture mode on its own — the matching marker beside it is then a second
		// statement of one fact rather than a requirement.
		if line.startsWith(MmTraceFence) 'mmTraceBlock'
			let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: i)
			traceCapture = SpecTraceCapture.pinned(TraceFamily.mm, trace: block.text)
			i = block.nextIndex
			continue
		end 'mmTraceBlock'

		if line.startsWith(LogTraceFence) 'logTraceBlock'
			let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: i)
			traceCapture = SpecTraceCapture.pinned(TraceFamily.log, trace: block.text)
			i = block.nextIndex
			continue
		end 'logTraceBlock'

		if line.startsWith(ExitCodeFence) 'exitCodeBlock'
			let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: i)
			let parsed = try int.fromString(block.text.trim()) otherwise 'badExitCode'
				i = block.nextIndex
				continue
			end 'badExitCode'
			exitCode = SpecExitCode.pinned(parsed)
			i = block.nextIndex
			continue
		end 'exitCodeBlock'

		if line.startsWith(SourceFence) 'source'
			let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: i)
			source = block.text
			haveSource = true
			i = block.nextIndex
			continue
		end 'source'

		// ⛔⛔ **THE TERMINATING ARM — A LIVE CASE MAY NOT OPEN A FENCE THIS PARSER DOES NOT IMPLEMENT.**
		// Every arm above consumes its whole block through `readFence`, so the only fence-opening line that
		// reaches here is one no arm claimed; the fallthrough below steps over it and then reads its BODY as
		// ordinary prose.
		//
		// That is not a harmless skip. It is this corpus's most expensive shape — a block that LOOKS like an
		// assertion and asserts nothing — and it was already here twice, both found by this arm the first
		// time it ran (2026-08-06, BATCH29/A3a). `vector.md` carried six ```RequiredIR blocks copied
		// byte-identically from `/specs`, pinning v1's instruction selection for an inlined `Vector.get`.
		// `tuples.md`'s `for-in-over-map-allocates-no-tuple` — an ACTIVE, PASSING case — carried the
		// ```mm-trace block that IS the property its name claims, and passed on its exit code alone.
		//
		// ⚠ The cure is not to implement every v1 fence: it is that dropping one becomes a decision somebody
		// makes and writes down. What shv2 pins instead is the minted fragment golden, which records what
		// THIS compiler emits rather than what another one did.
		//
		// ⚖ ONLY A LIVE CASE. A `disabled-test:` marker ALREADY says the case runs nowhere, so a block it
		// carries claims nothing — it is a record kept for the rung that will enable it, which is exactly
		// what the disabled form is for. Refusing there would delete the notes instead of the lies.
		if active and isUnimplementedFenceOpen(fenceLine) 'unimplementedFence'
			panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}:{i + 1}: live test '{name}' opens a fence this parser does not implement: '{fenceLine}'. The arms above are the whole set it can read, so this block is silently skipped: it pins NOTHING while reading as coverage. Delete it (what pins the emitted code here is the minted fragment golden), implement the fence, or shelve the case as `disabled-test:` naming the rung that unlocks it")
		end 'unimplementedFence'

		i = i + 1
	end 'blocks'

	// A DISABLED case has now consumed exactly the blocks a live one would have, which was
	// the entire point of scanning it; it contributes no test. A case with no source block
	// is not runnable either.
	if not active or not haveSource 'notRunnable'
		return i
	end 'notRunnable'

	// A ```maxoncstderr block WINS over an ```exitcode one: the two are mutually exclusive,
	// and a spec carrying both is asserting the compile FAILS — which no exit code of the
	// program it never produced could also be true of.
	if compilerError.isPinned() 'rejected'
		// ⭐⭐ **A REJECTED CASE PRODUCES NO BINARY, so every block that reads one is refused here
		// rather than silently discarded** — and silently discarded is exactly what happened: a
		// ```RequiredRdata claiming bytes no image could hold rode along beside a ```maxoncstderr and
		// the case PASSED, because `SpecExpectation.compilerError` has nowhere to carry a section pin
		// and the compile that would have been read never linked. A ```RequiredRuntime is the same
		// story one flag over: the runner still spells `--emit-ir-runtime=` onto the command line, but
		// the compile fails before the printer runs, so neither the render nor the printer's own
		// misspelling refusal ever happens.
		if sectionPins.count() > 0 or runtimeNames.count() > 0 'pinsOnARejectedCase'
			panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}: test '{name}' expects a COMPILE ERROR (a ```maxoncstderr block) and also carries a {RequiredDataFence}/{RequiredRdataFence}/{RequiredRuntimeFence} block. A case that never links produces no image to read a section out of and no IR to render a body into, so that block cannot fail and pins nothing. Drop it, or split the pin into a case that compiles")
		end 'pinsOnARejectedCase'

		// ⛔ A capture block on a REJECTED case is refused for the reason the section pins beside it are:
		// a compile that fails produces no binary, so there is no program to run under the monitor and no
		// trace the block could ever be compared against. It would pin nothing while reading as coverage.
		match traceCapture 'traceCaptureOnARejectedCase'
			off then break 'traceCaptureOnARejectedCase'
			unpinned to pinned then panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}: test '{name}' expects a COMPILE ERROR (a ```maxoncstderr block) and also carries a `{traceFenceOf(traceCaptureFamilyOf(traceCapture))}` block or `{traceMarkerOf(traceCaptureFamilyOf(traceCapture))} -->` marker. A case that never links produces no program to run under the monitor, so that block cannot fail and pins nothing. Drop it, or split the trace into a case that compiles")
		end 'traceCaptureOnARejectedCase'

		tests.push(SpecTest.create(name, source: source, expectation: SpecExpectation.compilerError(compilerError.resolved()), targets: targets, runtimeNames: runtimeNames, programArgs: programArgs, asyncTrace: asyncTrace, traceCapture: traceCapture, network: network))
		return i
	end 'rejected'

	// Whatever result blocks this case carried are ONE claim about ONE execution. Each stream's union is
	// built HERE, from its resolved slot, so no reader downstream can resolve the pair a second and
	// different way. Two constructors rather than one because `SpecStdout` and `SpecStderr` are distinct
	// types, each naming ITS stream; everything above the constructor is already shared.
	let resolvedStdout = SpecStdout.pinned(stdout.resolved()) if stdout.isPinned() else SpecStdout.unpinned
	let resolvedStderr = SpecStderr.pinned(stderr.resolved()) if stderr.isPinned() else SpecStderr.unpinned
	if not pinsAnyResult(exitCode, stderr: resolvedStderr, stdout: resolvedStdout, sectionPins: sectionPins, traceCapture: traceCapture) 'pinsNothing'
		panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}: test '{name}' has a ```maxon block but no result block — add an ```exitcode, ```stdout, ```stderr, ```maxoncstderr, ```RequiredData or ```RequiredRdata block, or shelve the case as `<!-- disabled-test: {name} -->`. A case with nothing to check is not a test, and running it would report PASS for a program nothing looked at. (A target-qualified block — `{TargetStdoutFencePrefix}<target>` and its two twins — counts only on the target it NAMES, so a case whose only result block names another target has none here)")
	end 'pinsNothing'

	tests.push(SpecTest.create(name, source: source, expectation: SpecExpectation.run(exitCode, stderr: resolvedStderr, stdout: resolvedStdout, sectionPins: sectionPins), targets: targets, runtimeNames: runtimeNames, programArgs: programArgs, asyncTrace: asyncTrace, traceCapture: traceCapture, network: network))

	return i
end 'scanTestFromMarker'

// ⚠ BOTH FUNCTIONS BELOW TAKE AN ALREADY-TRIMMED LINE, and the caller trims once for the pair. Each used
// to decide for itself whether the line "opens a fence" and they decided DIFFERENTLY — one trimming, one
// not — which is this file's own subject arriving in the guard that closes it: an indented unreadable
// fence satisfied neither and was skipped in silence. The argument type is what keeps them agreeing.

// Whether `trimmedLine` opens a fence whose info string carries WHITESPACE — i.e. a prose sentence
// starting with three backticks rather than one of this grammar's fences, every one of which is a single
// token. Asked BEFORE the arms, because it is the arms' prefix matching that would misread it.
//
// The delimiter itself holds no space, so in a trimmed line any space or tab is in the info string.
function fenceOpenCarriesProse(trimmedLine String) returns bool
	if not trimmedLine.startsWith(FenceDelimiter) 'notAFence'
		return false
	end 'notAFence'

	return trimmedLine.contains(" ") or trimmedLine.contains("\t")
end 'fenceOpenCarriesProse'

// Whether `trimmedLine` opens a block `scanTestFromMarker` has no reader for — asked only AFTER every
// fence arm has declined it, so a tag with an arm never reaches here.
//
// The two exceptions are PROSE and make no claim, which is why walking past them is correct and always
// was: a bare fence (the closing delimiter of any block is the same three bytes, and 11,210 lines in this
// corpus are one or the other) and `ProseFenceOpen`.
function isUnimplementedFenceOpen(trimmedLine String) returns bool
	if not trimmedLine.startsWith(FenceDelimiter) 'notAFence'
		return false
	end 'notAFence'

	return trimmedLine != FenceDelimiter and trimmedLine != ProseFenceOpen
end 'isUnimplementedFenceOpen'

// Consume a section-pin fence opening at `openIndex` and record what it claims about `section`,
// returning the line index to resume the block scan from. THE one place a pin is read, so the two
// fences cannot come to disagree about how a block becomes a claim.
//
// ⚠ Pins ACCUMULATE, which is why this pushes rather than assigns: a case may pin both sections, and
// a second block of the same kind is still a second claim about the same link rather than a
// replacement for the first.
function pushSectionPin(lines StringArray, openIndex LineIndex, testName String, fence String, section LinkedSectionKind, pins SpecSectionPinArray) returns LineIndex
	let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: openIndex)
	let text = block.text.trim()

	// A section pin is compared as a PREFIX (`SpecTestRunner.compareLinkedSection`), and the empty
	// prefix is a prefix of EVERY image — so a block with no typed values is a claim no binary can
	// fail. See `refuseVacuousDirective`.
	if text.isEmpty() 'noValues'
		refuseVacuousDirective(testName, fence: fence, remedy: "write the typed values it claims (`i64 42`, `i8 1`, `f64 3.14`, one per line), or delete the block. A section pin is compared as a PREFIX, and an EMPTY prefix matches every image ever linked — it cannot even say the section is empty")
	end 'noValues'

	pins.push(SpecSectionPin.create(section, text: text))
	return block.nextIndex
end 'pushSectionPin'

// Consume a ```RequiredRuntime fence and record the emitted-function names it lists — one per line,
// blank lines skipped, exactly as `parseTypedSectionValues` skips them in a section pin.
//
// ⚠ Names ACCUMULATE across blocks, for `pushSectionPin`'s reason and one more: this used to ASSIGN,
// so a case carrying two ```RequiredRuntime blocks silently rendered only the second one's bodies
// while reading as though it had asked for both.
function pushRuntimeNames(lines StringArray, openIndex LineIndex, testName String, names RuntimeFunctionNameArray) returns LineIndex
	let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: openIndex)

	// What THIS block contributed is the difference the accumulator itself reports — not a counter
	// beside it, which would be a second tally of one fact and free to disagree with the pushes.
	let before = names.count()

	for rawLine in block.text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		let name = rawLine.trim()
		if not name.isEmpty() 'nonBlank'
			names.push(name)
		end 'nonBlank'
	end 'eachLine'

	// ⚠ **THE REFUSAL THE COMPILER'S OWN CANNOT REACH.** `TargetPrinter.requireEveryNameRendered`
	// catches a MISSPELLED name, but it only ever runs on a `--emit-ir-runtime=` the runner emitted —
	// and the runner emits nothing for a block that listed nothing. An empty block therefore renders
	// the same golden a test WITHOUT the block renders, for ever, while claiming a gate on an emitted
	// body. See `refuseVacuousDirective`.
	if names.count() == before 'noNames'
		refuseVacuousDirective(testName, fence: RequiredRuntimeFence, remedy: "name the emitted functions it renders (`__str_bytes_view`, `mrt_start`, one per line), or delete the block. An empty one renders no body at all, so the golden is byte-identical to a test that never asked")
	end 'noNames'

	return block.nextIndex
end 'pushRuntimeNames'

// ⭐⭐ **A DIRECTIVE THAT PINS NOTHING IS REFUSED, and it is `pinsAnyResult`'s rule aimed one level
// down.** That guard asks whether a case pinned ANY result and panics when it pinned none; this asks
// whether each block a case DID write can fail, because a block that cannot fail is worse than an
// absent one on both counts — it claims a gate the case does not have, AND it satisfies the guard
// that would otherwise have caught the case for having no gate at all. (Measured, not feared: a case
// whose only block was an empty ```RequiredRdata passed `pinsAnyResult` and then passed its own pin,
// checking literally nothing.)
//
// It panics rather than skipping the case for the reason `pinsNothing` panics: a spec-authoring
// mistake that DOWNGRADED itself to a skip would be the same silence one step quieter.
function refuseVacuousDirective(testName String, fence String, remedy String)
	panic("SpecParser: test '{testName}' carries a {fence} block that pins NOTHING — {remedy}. A block that no binary can fail is not a gate, and it also satisfies the `pinsAnyResult` guard that exists to catch a case with nothing to check")
end 'refuseVacuousDirective'

// Does this case assert ANYTHING about the run? The push condition, and the one place the four halves of
// a run expectation are asked as a group — a fifth half added to `SpecExpectation.run` without a clause
// here would be a result block that still counts as no result.
function pinsAnyResult(exitCode SpecExitCode, stderr SpecStderr, stdout SpecStdout, sectionPins SpecSectionPinArray, traceCapture SpecTraceCapture) returns bool
	// A capture GOLDEN is a result check; the bare MARKER is not — it is a request for one, and a case
	// carrying only that has nothing to compare until `--update-required` has run. Both are capture mode,
	// and only one of them is an assertion, which is the distinction the three-state field exists to make.
	match traceCapture 'trace'
		off to unpinned then break 'trace'
		pinned then return true
	end 'trace'
	match exitCode 'exit'
		unpinned then break 'exit'
		pinned then return true
	end 'exit'
	match stderr 'err'
		unpinned then break 'err'
		pinned then return true
	end 'err'
	match stdout 'out'
		unpinned then break 'out'
		pinned then return true
	end 'out'
	return sectionPins.count() > 0
end 'pinsAnyResult'

// Consume a PORTABLE result fence opening at `openIndex` and record its text, returning the line index to
// resume the block scan from. Its target-qualified twin is below, and the pair is why the three portable
// arms are three lines each instead of the same five lines written out three times: what differs between
// a ```maxoncstderr, a ```stderr and a ```stdout block is WHICH SLOT it lands in, and nothing else.
function recordPortableText(lines StringArray, openIndex LineIndex, slot TargetPreferredText) returns LineIndex
	let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: openIndex)
	slot.recordPortable(block.text.trim())
	return block.nextIndex
end 'recordPortableText'

// Consume a TARGET-QUALIFIED result fence opening at `openIndex` and record its text into `slot` when —
// and only when — the fence names THIS run's target, returning the line index to resume the block scan
// from. THE one place a fence's `:<target>` suffix is compared against the run's target, shared by all
// three qualified fences: a second spelling of "does this block name my target" is one fact written twice.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY such block is READ, whatever target it names.** The fence has to be CONSUMED either way, or
// the outer scan walks its contents line by line as markup — and those contents are arbitrary text: a
// `## ` line inside one ENDS the test at that point, so an unconsumed `` ```Maxoncstderr:arm64-macos ``
// sitting above a case's ```maxon block silently deletes the whole case from the run. (Measured on the
// tree that had no such arm: the case did not fail, it simply was not in the count.) Only the block naming
// this run's target is KEPT.
function recordTargetQualifiedText(lines StringArray, openIndex LineIndex, openLine String, fencePrefix String, effectiveTargetKey String, slot TargetPreferredText) returns LineIndex
	let block = readFence(lines, openIndex: openIndex)

	if valueAfterKey(openLine, key: fencePrefix) == effectiveTargetKey 'namesThisTarget'
		slot.recordQualified(block.text.trim())
	end 'namesThisTarget'

	return block.nextIndex
end 'recordTargetQualifiedText'

// The two section lists one ```maxon block yields. A record rather than two calls over the same text:
// the scan is one pass and the two answers come out of the same walk, so a second walk would be a
// second thing free to disagree about where a section ends.
type SpecSourceSections
	export let files as SpecSourceFileArray
	export let stdlibOverlays as SpecSourceFileArray

	static function create(files SpecSourceFileArray, stdlibOverlays SpecSourceFileArray) returns SpecSourceSections
		return Self{files: files, stdlibOverlays: stdlibOverlays}
	end 'create'
end 'SpecSourceSections'

// Split a ```maxon block into its `// --- file:` and `// --- stdlib-overlay:` sections. BOTH lists are
// EMPTY when the block has no marker — the ordinary single-file test, which is most of them.
//
// A faithful port of the C# reference's `SplitMultiFileSource` (`SpecParser.cs:273`), including
// the two things about it that are easy to get subtly different and would silently misreport a
// diagnostic's line for ever after:
//   • the text BEFORE the first marker is DISCARDED (a block that opens with prose or a stray
//     blank line loses it, and no file inherits it);
//   • each section is TRIMMED, but its line OFFSET is measured from the line right after its
//     marker — not from its first non-blank line. Both compilers therefore agree on what line a
//     multi-file diagnostic sits on, which is the only property that matters here, and it is what
//     makes a `/specs` expectation portable byte-for-byte.
//
// ⚠ The overlay marker is shv2's own and the reference has no counterpart, so it takes the SAME
// offset arithmetic rather than a second one: an overlay's text is written at the top of the staged
// copy, so its own line 1 is that file's line 1, which is exactly the relation a `// --- file:`
// section's staged file already has.
function splitSourceSections(testName String, source String) returns SpecSourceSections
	if not FileMarker.startsWith(SectionMarkerPrefix) or not StdlibOverlayMarker.startsWith(SectionMarkerPrefix) 'markersDrifted'
		panic("SpecParser.splitSourceSections: '{FileMarker}' and '{StdlibOverlayMarker}' must both begin with '{SectionMarkerPrefix}', because the misspelled-marker refusal below recognizes a candidate by that prefix alone. One of them was renamed and the guard now admits a shape neither marker has")
	end 'markersDrifted'

	var files = SpecSourceFileArray.create()
	var overlays = SpecSourceFileArray.create()
	let lines = source.split("\n")
	let lineCount = lines.count()

	var name = ""
	var body = ""
	var isOverlay = false
	var haveMarker = false
	var offset = 0 as LineIndex

	for i in 0 upto lineCount 'eachLine'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.splitSourceSections: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the upto bound")

		let opensFile = line.startsWith(FileMarker)
		if not opensFile and not line.startsWith(StdlibOverlayMarker) 'notAMarker'
			// ⭐ A line WEARING the marker shape but matching neither is a typo, and the failure mode of
			// letting it through is silence: it becomes a comment inside whichever section precedes it,
			// the section it meant to open never exists, and the case then reports on a program the
			// author did not write. Refused where the shape is recognized.
			if line.startsWith(SectionMarkerPrefix) 'misspelledMarker'
				panic("SpecParser: test '{testName}' carries the line '{line.trim()}', which has the shape of a section marker but is neither '{FileMarker}' nor '{StdlibOverlayMarker}'. A marker the parser does not recognize is kept as SOURCE, so the section it names is never staged and the case checks a program nobody wrote — fix the spelling, or write the line as ordinary source without the '{SectionMarkerPrefix}' opening")
			end 'misspelledMarker'

			if haveMarker 'insideASection'
				body = "{body}\n{line}"
			end 'insideASection'
			continue
		end 'notAMarker'

		if haveMarker 'flushPrevious'
			pushSection(files, overlays: overlays, isOverlay: isOverlay, name: name, body: body, offset: offset)
		end 'flushPrevious'

		isOverlay = not opensFile
		name = valueAfterKey(line, key: FileMarker if opensFile else StdlibOverlayMarker)
		body = ""
		haveMarker = true
		// This section's first line is the one AFTER the marker: 0-based block index `i + 1`, which is
		// block LINE NUMBER `i + 2`, which is fragment line number `i + 2 + FragmentHeaderLines - 1`.
		// The section's own line 1 must land there, so the offset added to a per-section line is that
		// minus one. (Checked against the corpus: `export-keyword`'s
		// `error.non-exported-function-cross-file` expects `:11:9`, and this yields exactly 11.)
		offset = i + FragmentHeaderLines + 1
	end 'eachLine'

	if haveMarker 'flushLast'
		pushSection(files, overlays: overlays, isOverlay: isOverlay, name: name, body: body, offset: offset)
	end 'flushLast'

	if overlays.count() > 0 and files.count() == 0 'overlayWithoutAFile'
		panic("SpecParser: test '{testName}' declares a '{StdlibOverlayMarker}' section but no '{FileMarker}' section. An overlay says what the compile's STDLIB is, never what it COMPILES, so there would be no program — and the block's own text cannot stand in for one, since it carries the marker lines themselves. Add the '{FileMarker}' section that holds the program")
	end 'overlayWithoutAFile'

	return SpecSourceSections.create(files, stdlibOverlays: overlays)
end 'splitSourceSections'

// File the section just closed into the list its marker chose. Split out because the scan closes a
// section in two places — at the next marker and at end of block — and the two must agree about which
// list, which trimming and which offset a section gets.
function pushSection(files SpecSourceFileArray, overlays SpecSourceFileArray, isOverlay bool, name String, body String, offset LineIndex)
	let section = SpecSourceFile.create(name, source: body.trim(), mergedLineOffset: offset)
	if isOverlay 'toOverlays'
		overlays.push(section)
	end 'toOverlays' else 'toFiles'
		files.push(section)
	end 'toFiles'
end 'pushSection'

// The VALUE of an HTML-comment marker line: the text between the first ": " and the trailing
// " -->", trimmed — i.e. NAME out of `<!-- test: NAME -->`, or the target list out of
// `<!-- targets: k1, k2 -->`. It reads any `<!-- key: value -->` marker, because the two share one
// grammar; a target key holds no ": ", so the first separator is always the marker's own.
//
// EXPORTED because the harness self-test's fixtures carry a marker in that same grammar
// (`<!-- expect-refusal: … -->`, HarnessSelfTest). A second reader of one marker syntax is exactly
// the drift this file's other shared constants exist to prevent.
export function markerValue(line String) returns String
	for opening in markerOpeningsIn(line) 'eachOpening'
		let body = markerBodyOf(opening)
		let colonIdx = try body.findFirst(MarkerNameSep) otherwise continue
		// Advance one index past the ':' rather than measuring the separator's length — a method call
		// directly on a top-level `let` constant is a parse error in the bootstrap. `trim()` then drops
		// whatever whitespace the author put on either side, which is the reference's `\s*` exactly.
		let valueStart = try body.indexAfter(colonIdx) otherwise continue
		return body.slice(valueStart, endIndex: body.endIndex()).trim()
	end 'eachOpening'

	return ""
end 'markerValue'

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY `<!--` ON A LINE, EACH NORMALIZED TO THE CANONICAL SPELLING — the ONE place that decides
// where a marker begins, and the one place the reference's `\s*` is spent.**
//
// Each opening comes back as `<!-- <trimmed text>`, which is exactly what the marker constants above
// ARE — so every question after this one is a plain `startsWith` against a constant, with no
// whitespace rule of its own and nothing to allocate. NORMALIZING THE OPENING RATHER THAN TAKING EACH
// MARKER CONSTANT APART is what makes the tolerance cost one allocation per LINE instead of one per
// (line × marker family): there are eight families, and `scanTestFromMarker` asks about all of them on
// every line of every case (measured 2026-08-24: the per-family form cost ~0.5 s of fixed overhead on
// a run that parses the corpus and exits).
//
// The three questions this file asks of a marker line — WHICH marker is it (`openingsCarry`), what is
// its VALUE (`markerValue`), and is it CLOSED (`refuseUnterminatedTestMarker`) — all read this one
// answer. A second walk is how they would come to disagree, and one of them already had: the refusal
// asked the whole LINE for a `-->` while the body asked one OPENING, so on `<!-- note --> <!-- test: x`
// the refusal was satisfied by the other comment's closer and the unterminated marker went through.
//
// The text before the first opening is not an opening, and a line carrying none yields none.
function markerOpeningsIn(line String) returns StringArray
	var openings = StringArray.create()
	var seenFirst = false
	for piece in line.split(CommentOpen) 'eachPiece'
		if not seenFirst 'textBeforeTheFirstOpening'
			seenFirst = true
			continue
		end 'textBeforeTheFirstOpening'

		openings.push("{CommentOpen} {piece.trim()}")
	end 'eachPiece'

	return openings
end 'markerOpeningsIn'

// What one opening SAYS: everything up to the `-->` that closes it, or the whole of it when nothing on
// this line does. The fallback is deliberate and is the older behaviour — an unterminated marker still
// yields a name, so `refuseUnterminatedTestMarker` can quote it back at the author.
function markerBodyOf(opening String) returns String
	let parts = opening.split(MarkerEnd)
	return try parts.get(0) otherwise panic("SpecParser.markerBodyOf: split yields at least one part for any input, so index 0 cannot be absent")
end 'markerBodyOf'

// ⭐ **DOES ONE OF THESE OPENINGS CARRY `marker`?** The openings are already canonical, so this is the
// plain prefix test the marker constants were always written to be — the whitespace the reference
// allows was spent once, in `markerOpeningsIn`.
//
// Takes the OPENINGS rather than the line so the caller can compute them once and ask about all eight
// families; `carriesMarker` below is the door for a caller that asks once.
function openingsCarry(openings StringArray, marker String) returns bool
	return match markerOpeningAmong(openings, marker: marker) 'opening'
		at gives true
		absent gives false
	end 'opening'
end 'openingsCarry'

// WHICH opening carries `marker`, not merely whether one does — the refusal needs the opening itself,
// because whether a marker is closed is a property of ITS `<!--`, never of the line it sits on.
union MarkerOpening
	at(text String)
	absent
end 'MarkerOpening'

function markerOpeningAmong(openings StringArray, marker String) returns MarkerOpening
	for opening in openings 'eachOpening'
		if opening.startsWith(marker) 'thisOpeningIsIt'
			return MarkerOpening.at(opening)
		end 'thisOpeningIsIt'
	end 'eachOpening'

	return MarkerOpening.absent
end 'markerOpeningAmong'

// The one-shot door: does `line` carry `marker`? For callers that ask about a single family, where
// computing the openings and asking are one act — `SpecTestRunner`'s two line walks, and the harness
// self-test's fixture reader. The scanners in this file hoist the openings instead, because they ask
// eight times per line.
export function carriesMarker(line String, marker String) returns bool
	return openingsCarry(markerOpeningsIn(line), marker: marker)
end 'carriesMarker'

// The target keys a `<!-- targets: k1, k2 -->` marker names — its value split on `,`, each part
// trimmed, empties dropped. An EMPTY result (a blank value) is "no restriction", which the runner
// treats exactly as a marker-less test. Comma is the shared spec-format separator (see `TargetsMarker`);
// this splits it the same way the C# and v1 parsers do so one `.md` reads identically in all three.
function parseTargetKeys(line String) returns TargetKeyArray
	var result = TargetKeyArray.create()
	let rawValue = markerValue(line)
	for part in rawValue.split(TargetSeparator) 'eachTarget'
		let trimmed = part.trim()
		if not trimmed.isEmpty() 'nonEmpty'
			result.push(trimmed)
		end 'nonEmpty'
	end 'eachTarget'
	return result
end 'parseTargetKeys'

// What a `<!-- network: … -->` marker says this case needs, read through the same `markerValue` every
// other valued marker goes through.
//
// ⛔ **AN UNRECOGNIZED VALUE IS A PANIC, NOT A `none`.** Both failing spellings are silent otherwise and
// both fail OPEN: a typo (`<!-- network: livee -->`) and a blank value would each be read as "reaches no
// host", so the case would rejoin the default gate and the suite would go red on somebody else's outage
// with nothing anywhere saying why. That is the same failure `AsyncTraceMarker` records having shipped —
// a marker the file states and the harness declines — arriving through a value rather than through a
// missing arm.
function parseNetworkValue(line String, specPath FilePath, testName String) returns SpecNetwork
	let value = markerValue(line)
	if value == NetworkLiveValue 'live'
		return SpecNetwork.live
	end 'live'

	panic("SpecParser: {specPath.toString()}: test '{testName}' carries `{NetworkMarker} {value} -->`, and '{NetworkLiveValue}' is the only value this marker takes. It says the case opens a socket to a REAL EXTERNAL HOST, so a default run leaves it out and `--network` brings it back; an unrecognized value would quietly read as 'reaches no host' and put the case back in the default gate")
end 'parseNetworkValue'

// The arguments an `<!-- Args: … -->` marker names, in order — its value split into the argv the
// program is spawned with. `<!-- Args: -5.5 -->` is ONE argument, `-5.5`: a value beginning with `-`
// is an argument like any other, never a flag, because nothing between here and the child's `main`
// interprets it (`runProcess` hands `Subprocess` an argv LIST, so no shell and no option parser ever
// sees these bytes).
//
// SPACE separates; a DOUBLE-QUOTED run is one argument and may be EMPTY. The quote is not decoration:
// `specs/command-line-args.md`'s `empty-string-arg` writes `<!-- Args: "" -->` and asserts the program
// receives ONE argument that is the empty string — a claim a plain space split cannot express at all
// (it yields the two-character token `""`). The C# reference gets this rule for free by handing its
// payload to the OS as a VERBATIM command line, which the platform then splits by exactly these rules;
// shv2's `Subprocess` takes an argv list, so the same rule is applied here instead of by the OS. One
// `.md`, one meaning, whichever compiler reads it.
//
// An UNTERMINATED quote runs to the end of the payload rather than being refused — again the platform's
// own rule, so the two compilers agree even on a directive neither spec author meant to write.
// Backslash escapes and single quotes are NOT honoured: nothing in `/specs` writes them, and the
// platform rule they would have to mirror is per-OS.
function parseProgramArgs(line String) returns ProgramArgArray
	var args = ProgramArgArray.create()
	let payload = markerValue(line)

	// The argument being accumulated, and whether one has been STARTED — which `current.isEmpty()`
	// cannot answer: `""` starts an argument and contributes no characters to it, and inferring the
	// flag from the buffer would drop exactly the empty argument the quote exists to write.
	var current = StringBuilder.create()
	var haveArg = false
	var inQuotes = false

	// The text since the last quote or separator. Accumulating by SLICE rather than character keeps
	// multi-byte characters whole without this walk having to know anything about them.
	var segmentStart = payload.startIndex()
	var pos = payload.startIndex()

	let length = payload.count()
	var at = 0 as GraphemeCount
	while at < length 'eachCharacter'
		let ch = payload.slice(pos, length: 1)
		let isQuote = ch == ArgQuote
		let isSeparator = not inQuotes and ch == ArgSeparator

		// Advanced ONCE, before the branches: both the next segment's start and the next character's
		// position are this same index, and computing it twice would be one fact — and one panic
		// message — written down twice.
		let next = try payload.indexAfter(pos) otherwise panic("SpecParser.parseProgramArgs: indexAfter failed at character {at} of {length} on the payload of '{line}', which the while guard says is still in range")

		if isQuote or isSeparator 'segmentEnds'
			current.append(payload.slice(segmentStart, endIndex: pos))
			segmentStart = next

			if isQuote 'toggleQuoting'
				inQuotes = not inQuotes
				haveArg = true
			end 'toggleQuoting' else 'argumentEnds'
				if haveArg 'flush'
					args.push(current.build())
					haveArg = false
				end 'flush'
			end 'argumentEnds'
		end 'segmentEnds' else 'ordinaryCharacter'
			haveArg = true
		end 'ordinaryCharacter'

		pos = next
		at = at + 1
	end 'eachCharacter'

	current.append(payload.slice(segmentStart, endIndex: payload.endIndex()))
	if haveArg 'flushLast'
		args.push(current.build())
	end 'flushLast'

	return args
end 'parseProgramArgs'

// Lines joined by "\n", with no leading and no trailing newline.
//
// The suite accumulates line-oriented text in exactly two places — a spec's fenced blocks
// (`readFence`, below) and a worker's whole stderr (`drainChildStderr`, SpecWorkerPool) — and both used
// to hand-roll this dance identically. Both are also UNBOUNDED in the input: a fence is a whole test
// program, a drain is everything a crashed child said. So both must accumulate into a `StringBuilder`,
// because a `String` owns exactly the bytes it holds and its `append` recopies all of them
// (stdlib/String.maxon), making a join Θ(n²) in its own output.
//
// `hasLine` is NOT `out.isEmpty()`. A first line that is itself EMPTY leaves the builder empty and must
// still be separated from the line after it — a spec fence opening on a blank line is ordinary, and
// inferring the flag from the buffer would silently eat that newline.
export type LineJoiner
	var out as StringBuilder
	var hasLine as bool

	export static function create() returns LineJoiner
		return LineJoiner{out: StringBuilder.create(), hasLine: false}
	end 'create'

	export function push(line String)
		if hasLine 'separator'
			out.append("\n")
		end 'separator'

		out.append(line)
		hasLine = true
	end 'push'

	/// The joined text. CONSUMING, as `StringBuilder.build` is: the joiner is empty afterwards.
	export function build() returns String
		hasLine = false
		return out.build()
	end 'build'
end 'LineJoiner'

// Read the fenced block whose opening ``` is at `openIndex`. Content lines are
// joined with "\n" and NO trailing newline, so a ```maxon block is reproduced
// VERBATIM — the runner prepends the one `// Test: <name>` line the C# reference
// prepends and nothing else, which is what puts a spec's code at the same fragment
// line number under both compilers (SpecTestRunner's `fragmentSource`). A missing
// closing fence treats EOF as the close (robust against a malformed spec rather
// than looping).
function readFence(lines StringArray, openIndex LineIndex) returns FenceBlock
	let lineCount = lines.count()
	var text = LineJoiner.create()
	var i = openIndex + 1

	while i < lineCount 'read'
		let line = try lines.get(i) otherwise panic("SpecParser.readFence: index {i} >= lineCount {lineCount} despite the while guard i<lineCount")

		if line.startsWith(FenceDelimiter) 'close'
			return FenceBlock.create(text.build(), nextIndex: i + 1)
		end 'close'

		// ⚠ **A CONTENT LINE THAT ENDS WITH THE CLOSING FENCE** — `42``` ` (the content `42` and the
		// closing ` ``` ` on ONE line, no newline between them). It is malformed markdown, but the bootstrap's
		// SpecParser tolerates it and the byte-identical corpus contains it (`specs/arrays.md`'s
		// `array-with-reserve` exitcode), so the port must too or that exitcode is misread and the block
		// scanner walks into the next test. The remainder before the fence is the final content line, then the
		// block closes. Only fires when the line is NOT itself a bare fence (handled above), and no other spec
		// has a genuine content line ending in ` ``` `, so this changes nothing else.
		if line.endsWith(FenceDelimiter) 'inlineClose'
			var stop = line.startIndex()
			for _ in 0 upto line.byteLength() - FenceDelimiter.byteLength() 'toFence'
				stop = try line.indexAfter(stop) otherwise panic("SpecParser.readFence: indexAfter failed before the inline closing fence — the byte length and the index walk disagree on an ASCII line")
			end 'toFence'
			text.push(line.slice(line.startIndex(), endIndex: stop))
			return FenceBlock.create(text.build(), nextIndex: i + 1)
		end 'inlineClose'

		text.push(line)
		i = i + 1
	end 'read'

	return FenceBlock.create(text.build(), nextIndex: lineCount)
end 'readFence'
